hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1201","977/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani and Abu Yasin narrated to us, both said: Yusuf narrated with it. I (Ibn Hajar) say: Yusuf is alone in it, and he is very weak."
"1202","978 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Bahr narrated to us, Abd al-Rahim ibn Zayd al-Ammi narrated, from his father, from al-Hasan, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever walks to the need of his Muslim brother, Allah the Exalted writes for him for every step he takes a good deed until he returns from where he parted; if his need is fulfilled he emerges from his sins as on the day his mother bore him, and if he perishes in between he enters Paradise without reckoning. Abd al-Rahim is very weak."
"1203","979/1 - He also said: Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us, al-Salt, meaning Ibn al-Hajjaj, narrated, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever helps his brother in his need and is gentle with him, it is incumbent upon Allah the Exalted to provide him servants from the servants of Paradise."
"1204","979/2 - Muhammad ibn Bahr narrated to us, al-Mu'alla ibn Maymun al-Mujashi'i narrated, Yazid al-Raqashi narrated, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is gentle with a believer, or sponsors him in any of his needs, small or great, and he mentioned the like of it. I (Ibn Hajar) say: Its pivot is upon Yazid, and he is weak."
"1205","980 - Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, Abd al-Hakam ibn Mansur narrated, Ziyad ibn Abi Hassan narrated, who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever rescues one in distress, Allah the Exalted writes for him seventy-three good deeds, one of them by which Allah the Exalted sets right for him his worldly and otherworldly affair, and seventy-two in ranks."
"1206","981 - Ubaydullah ibn Umar ibn Maysarah narrated to us, al-Sakan ibn Isma'il narrated, Ziyad ibn Maymun narrated, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The guide to good is like its doer, and Allah the Exalted loves rescuing the distressed. I (Ibn Hajar) say: Ziyad ibn Abi Hassan is Ziyad ibn Maymun, abandoned."
"1207","982 - Jubarah, that is Ibn al-Mughallis, narrated to us, Abd al-Samad ibn al-Azraq narrated, Sukayn ibn Abi Siraj informed me, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Maymun ibn Mihran, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Who are the best of people? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most beneficial of them to people."
"1208","983 - Mus'ab al-Zubayri narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Amir, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from Zayd ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with them) who said: Allah the Exalted remains in the need of the servant as long as the servant is in the need of his brother. He narrates that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1209","Chapter of condemning miserliness (36). 984 - Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so has a palm tree in my garden; order him to sell it or give it to me. He said: The man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Do it, for you is a palm tree in Paradise in return; but he refused. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is the most miserly of people."
"1210","Chapter of fulfilling promises (37). 985 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, from a woman, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for something and it was not available, so she said: O Messenger of Allah, promise me. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A promise is a gift."
"1211","Chapter of restraining the guest from burdening the host (38). 986 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Mansur narrated to us, Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated, Sulayman ibn Qarm narrated, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, who said: I went with a companion of mine to Salman (may Allah be pleased with him), and he brought bread and salt, so my companion said: If only there were thyme in our salt. So Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) sent his water vessel and brought thyme. When we ate, my companion said: Praise be to Allah who sufficed us with what He provided us. So Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Had you been sufficed, my water vessel would not be pawned."
"1212","Book of Fasting (11). Chapter of the month being twenty-nine (1). 987 - Ishaq: Abdullah ibn Waqid al-Jazari informed us, who said: I heard al-Awza'i narrate, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The month is twenty-nine."
"1213","Chapter of fasting upon sighting (2). 988 - Al-Harith: Dawud narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from Hajjaj, from al-Hasan, who said: Abdullah ibn Yazid addressed the people at the season, and said: O people, we have witnessed the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard from them, and they narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fast upon seeing the crescent, and break fast upon seeing it; if it is hidden from you, complete the number thirty days; and if two just witnesses testify, fast upon their sighting, break fast upon it, and withhold upon it."
"1214","Chapter of forbidding preceding Ramadan by a day or two (3). 989 - Al-Harith: Dawud narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: They said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Why do you not go ahead and add a day or two? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry."
"1215","Chapter of delaying Maghrib prayer to watch for the crescent on the night of fasting or breaking fast (4). 990 - Al-Harith: Abu Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Haywah narrated, from Ibn Shihab, who said: The Sunnah on a night when the crescent of Ramadan is awaited for fasting or breaking fast: the call to Maghrib prayer is made at its time, then the iqamah is delayed until the crescent is seen, or hope of it is lost, and some stars appear."
"1216","Chapter of two months not both being complete (5). 991 - Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Abu Shaybah, from Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Uqbah, from his father, from Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Two months do not complete sixty days. Abu Shaybah is Ibrahim ibn Uthman, grandfather of Abu Bakr, weak."
"1217","992 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Rukayn, from Husayn ibn Qabisah, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The month is thirty, and the month is twenty-nine."
"1218","993 - Al-Harith said: Rawh narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated, who said: I heard Simak say: I heard Abdullah ibn Shaddad and Ikrimah narrate that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The month is twenty-nine."
"1219","994 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Rawh narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated, Ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me, from a man of Banu Tamim whom we do not accuse of lying, who said: I informed A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) says: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The month is twenty-nine. So she disapproved of that and said: May Allah forgive Abu Abd al-Rahman; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not say it like that, but he said: The month is sometimes twenty-nine."
"1220","Chapter of the sign of the crescent belonging to its night (6). 995 - Abu Ya'la: Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Ras'ani narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated, Baqiyyah narrated, from Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the crescent sets before the twilight, it is for its night, and when it sets after the twilight, it is for two nights."
"1221","Chapter of what is said upon seeing the crescent (7). 996 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us from Abu Bishr, from Abbad ibn Ja'far al-Makhzumi who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw the crescent he would say: I believe in Allah who created you - three times."
"1222","Chapter of accepting Bedouins' testimony in fasting and breaking fast (8). 997 - Al-Harith said: Rawh narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated who said: I heard Mansur narrate, from Rib'i ibn Hirash, that two Bedouins testified before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they had seen the crescent yesterday for breaking fast or sacrifice, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted their testimony. This is mursal with a sound chain."
"1223","Chapter of the merit of fasting (9). 998 - Abu Bakr said: Affan narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from Uthman al-Batti, from Nu'aym ibn Abi Hind, from Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I was leaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) against my chest, and he said: Whoever fasts a day seeking the Face of Allah the Exalted, and it seals his end, enters Paradise."
"1224","999 - Abd ibn Humayd said: Rawh narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated, from Makhul, from Amr ibn Abasah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah is distanced from the Fire a journey of a hundred years."
"1225","1000 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Za'idah ibn Qudamah narrated, from al-A'mash, from Shimr ibn Atiyyah, from Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah Mighty and Majestic, Allah places between him and the Fire a trench as wide as between heaven and earth."
"1226","1001 - Al-Harith said: Abu al-Hasan Bishr ibn Abi Bishr al-Basri narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Abd al-Wahid al-Harrani informed me, Hayyan al-Basri narrated, from Ishaq ibn Nuh, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), turning to Usamah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with them both), say: O Usamah, upon you is the path of Paradise, and beware of being cut off short of it. He said: O Messenger of Allah, how quickly is that path traversed? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Thirst in midday heat, and restraining the soul from the pleasure of women. O Usamah, upon you is fasting, for it draws near to Allah Mighty and Majestic; there is nothing dearer to Allah the Exalted than the breath of the fasting person's mouth; he left food and drink for Allah the Exalted, so if you can that death comes to you while your stomach is hungry and your liver thirsty, then do so, for you will thereby attain honorable station in the Hereafter, and settle with the Prophets, rejoicing at your soul's arrival to them, and the Compeller will bless you. Then he mentioned the hadith, and in it: And know, O Usamah, that the nearest of people to Allah the Exalted on the Day of Resurrection are those whose grief, thirst, and hunger were longest in this world. And it will come, Allah willing, complete in al-Zuhd."
"1227","1002 - Abu Ya'la said: Bishr ibn Hilal narrated to us, Abd al-Warith narrated, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If a man fasted a voluntary day, then were given the earth full of gold, he would not fully receive its reward short of the Reckoning."
"1228","1003 - He also said: Ahmad ibn Isa narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to me, from Zabban ibn Fa'id, who said: Lahi'ah narrated to him from Amr ibn Rabi'ah, from Salamah ibn Qaysar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts a day seeking the Face of Allah the Exalted, Allah distances him from Hell like the distance of a crow that flew as a chick until it died of old age."
"1229","1004 - Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Isa narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Zabban ibn Fa'id, from Sahl ibn Mu'adh, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts a voluntary day in the path of Allah the Exalted outside Ramadan is distanced from the Fire a hundred years, the pace of a trained swift horse."
"1230","1005 - Abu Ya'la said: Hafs ibn Abdullah al-Hulwani narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Habib ibn Ubayd, from Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Everything has a door, and the door of worship is fasting."
"1231","Chapter of the merit of Ramadan (10). 1006 - Al-Harith said: Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, one of our companions called Iyas narrated to me, raising the hadith to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Salman al-Farisi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us on the last day of Sha'ban and said: O people, a blessed month has overshadowed you, in it a night better than a thousand months; Allah has made its fasting obligatory and made standing its night voluntary. Whoever performs a voluntary good trait in it is like one who performed an obligation in other than it, and whoever performs an obligation in it is like one who performed seventy obligations. It is the month of patience, and patience's reward is Paradise; it is the month of sympathy; it is a month in which the believer's provision is increased. Whoever breaks a fasting person's fast in it has freeing of a neck and forgiveness of his sins. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, not all of us find what to break a fasting person's fast with. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted gives this reward to whoever breaks a fasting person's fast with a sip of milk, or a date, or a drink of water; and whoever satiates a fasting person has forgiveness of his sins, and Allah Mighty and Majestic gives him to drink from my Basin a drink after which he will not thirst until he enters Paradise, and he has the like of his reward without diminishing his reward at all. It is a month whose beginning is mercy, middle is forgiveness, and end is freeing from the Fire; and whoever lightens for his slave in it, Allah frees him from the Fire."
"1232","1007 - Abu Ya'la said: Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hibban narrated to us, al-Fadl ibn al-Ala' narrated, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated, from al-Fadl ibn Isa, from his uncle Yazid ibn Aban, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: This is the month of Ramadan come; the gates of heaven are opened in it, the gates of the Fire are closed in it, and the devils are chained in it. Whoever reaches Ramadan and is not forgiven in it, when will he be forgiven? This is a weak hadith."
"1233","1008 - He also said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ar'arah narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, Amr ibn Hamzah Abu Usayd narrated, Khalaf Abu al-Rabi' narrated: Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said when Ramadan came: Glory be to Allah, what are you facing, or what is man facing - three times. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah, has revelation descended? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Has an enemy come? He said: No. He said: What then? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted forgives on the first night of Ramadan all the people of the qiblah. Then he looked at a man sitting before him moving his head and saying: Excellent, excellent. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As if your chest is constricted. He said: No, but I remembered the hypocrites. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The hypocrite is the disbeliever, and the disbeliever has no share of that. Ibn Khuzaymah transmitted it, saying: If the report is sound, for I do not know Khalaf nor Amr ibn Hamzah al-Qaysi by probity or disparagement."
"1234","1009 - Ahmad ibn Mani' and al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah both said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hisham ibn Abi Hisham narrated, from Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Aswad, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My nation has been given five traits in Ramadan not given to any nation before them: the breath of the fasting person's mouth is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk; the angels seek forgiveness for them until they break fast; Allah adorns His Garden every day then says: My fasting servants are about to cast off burden and harm and come to you; the rebellious devils are chained in it, not reaching in it what they reached in other than it; and they are forgiven in the last night. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, is it the Night of Decree? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, but the worker is only paid his wage when he completes his work. This is a weak chain."
"1235","1010 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi Saminah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated, Jarir ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from al-Sha'bi, from Nafi' ibn Burdah, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say when Ramadan began: If the servants knew what is in Ramadan, my nation would wish the whole year were Ramadan. A man of Khuza'ah said: Narrate it to us. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Paradise is adorned in Ramadan from year's start to year's start, until when it is the first day of Ramadan a wind blows from beneath the Throne and rustles the leaves of Paradise; the wide-eyed houris look at that and say: O Lord, make for us in this noble month husbands whose eyes delight in them and whose eyes delight in us. There is no servant who fasts Ramadan except that he is married to a wife of the wide-eyed houris in a tent of hollow pearl of what Allah the Exalted described: wide-eyed ones sheltered in tents; upon each woman of them seventy robes, no robe of the color of another; and he is given seventy kinds of perfume, none of the scent of another; for each woman of them seventy beds of red ruby studded with pearls; upon each bed seventy mattresses whose linings are of brocade; above the seventy mattresses seventy couches; for each woman a thousand maidservants for her needs, and a thousand menservants, with each menservant a golden dish in it a kind of food, finding in its last morsel a pleasure not found in its first; and her husband is given the like of that upon a bed of red ruby, upon him two bracelets of gold set with red ruby. This is for every day of Ramadan besides what good deeds he did. I (Ibn Hajar) say: Jarir ibn Ayyub is alone in it, and he is very weak. Ibn Khuzaymah transmitted it in his Sahih, saying: If the report is sound, for there is doubt in my heart about Jarir ibn Ayyub, as if he was lenient with it being of encouragements. And Ibn Mas'ud is not the famous Hudhali, but another Ghifari."
"1236","1011 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbih narrated, from Abu A'ishah, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, and he mentioned the hadith, and in it: Whoever fasts Ramadan and refrains from backbiting, tale-bearing, lying, delving into falsehood, withholds his tongue except from remembrance of Allah the Exalted, restrains his hearing, sight, and all limbs from Allah the Exalted's prohibitions and from harming Muslims, has of nearness to Allah the Exalted that his knee touches the knee of Ibrahim the Friend (peace and blessings be upon him). This is a fabricated hadith."
"1237","Chapter of requiring intention at night for the obligatory fast, not the voluntary (11). 1012 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Hilal narrated, from his father, that he heard Maymunah bint Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with her) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever resolves the fast at night, let him fast; and whoever wakes without having resolved it, let him not fast."
"1238","1013 - Musaddad said: Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, Layth narrated, from Mujahid, from one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would come and ask for food, not find it, and so make fasting obligatory upon himself. She said: And sometimes he would come while fasting and I had something nice, and we would say: O Messenger of Allah, had you not been fasting I would have fed you, so he would call and eat (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1239","1014 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abu Sufyan, I heard a man ask Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I took suhur then it occurred to me to break fast? He said: Break fast. Then he said: Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) would come to his family and say: Do you have anything? If they said: No, he said: Then I am fasting."
"1240","1015 - Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, Umm al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated to me, who said: Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) would come to them after morning and ask them for breakfast, not find it, and say: Then I am fasting. Sound, halted."
"1241","Chapter of what is avoided in fasting (12). 1016 - Al-Tayalisi said: A shaykh of the people of Makkah, meaning Talhah ibn Amr, narrated to us, from Ata', from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Guard fasting, for fasting is not merely from food and drink, but fasting is from sins."
"1242","1017 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muslim al-Abdi, from Abu al-Mutawakkil, who said: When Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) fasted he would sit in the mosque and say: We guard our fasting."
"1243","1018 - Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, who said: Our companions used to say: The easiest fasting is leaving food and drink."
"1244","1019 - He said: And Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated, from al-Hasan, who said: If vomit overcomes him he does not break fast, and if he vomits deliberately he breaks fast."
"1245","Chapter of one who said: Only food and drink break fast (13). 1020/1 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Rabi' narrated to us, Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated, my father narrated to me, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The sky rained hail, so Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) said to us while we were boys: Hand me, O Anas, some of that hail. So I handed him, and he ate while fasting. I said: Are you not fasting? He said: Yes indeed; this is not food nor drink, but a blessing from heaven with which we purify our stomachs. Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he said: Take from your uncle. This is a weak chain."
"1246","1020/2 - He said: And Abd al-Warith ibn Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, my father narrated with it."
"1247","1020/3 - Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Ma'mar narrated to us, Abd al-Samad narrated, and he mentioned it."
"1248","1020/4 - Al-Bazzar transmitted it from Hilal ibn Yahya, from Abu Awanah, from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I saw Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned it halted."
"1249","Chapter of the Sunnah of breaking fast on dates, fresh dates, or what fire has not touched (14). 1021 - Al-Harith said: Rawh narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated, I was told from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would fast in summer, not praying Maghrib in summer while fasting until I brought him fresh dates, so he would eat and drink, then stand and pray; and in winter I would bring him dates, so he would eat and drink, then stand and pray. In it is a break."
"1250","1022 - Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid narrated, Thabit narrated, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) loved to break fast on three dates, or something untouched by fire."
"1251","1023 - Abd ibn Humayd said: Abd al-Malik ibn Amr, that is Abu Amir al-Aqadi, narrated to us, Zam'ah ibn Salih narrated, from Muhammad ibn Abi Sulayman, from some of the family of Jabir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When there were fresh dates, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not break fast except on fresh dates, and when there were no fresh dates, he would not break fast except on dates."
"1252","Chapter thereof, and in it the Sunnah of hastening iftar and forbidding wisal (15). 1024 - Abu Ya'la said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, Tayyib ibn Sulayman narrated, who said: I heard Amrah, who said: I heard A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid wisal, and command hastening iftar and delaying suhur."
"1253","1025 - He also said: Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Musa ibn Isma'il narrated, Hubabah bint Ajlan narrated, from her mother Umm Hafs, from Safiyyah bint Jarir, from Umm Hakim bint Wada' who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Hasten iftar and delay suhur."
"1254","1026/1 - Al-Tayalisi said: Kharijah ibn Mus'ab narrated to us, from Haram ibn Uthman, from Abu Atiq, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no wisal in fasting."
"1255","1026/2 - He said: And al-Yaman Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, from Abu Atiq, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) with it."
"1256","1027 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Muslim ibn Babak narrated, from Ibn Aqil, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to practice wisal."
"1257","1028/1 - Al-Harith said: Isma'il ibn Abi Isma'il narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Haram ibn Uthman, from Abu Atiq, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No silence from day to night, no wisal in fasting, no returning to Bedouin life after migration, and no migration after the Conquest - the hadith. And Abu Qurrah narrated it from Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was informed from Haram ibn Uthman with it."
"1258","1029/1 - Al-Tayalisi said: Hammad narrated to us, from Bishr, that is Ibn Harb, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wisal, yet this sister of mine practices wisal, and I forbid her."
"1259","1029/2 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Nasr, that is al-Tammar, narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated with it."
"1260","Chapter of the concession to make up Ramadan at leisure (16). 1030 - Musaddad said: Sallam ibn Abi Muti' narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from his father, who said: A man asked Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) about making up Ramadan in the ten of Dhu al-Hijjah. He said: I do not know what the exchange between them was, but he ordered him to make up Ramadan in the ten of Dhu al-Hijjah. He said: And do not say that your father heard that from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1261","1031/1 - Musaddad also said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from a man of the descendants of Rafi' ibn Khadij, from his grandmother, who said: Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered her to make up Ramadan separately."
"1262","1031/2 - Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated, from Abd al-Majid ibn Rafi', from his grandmother, who said: Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: Count the number and fast as you wish."
"1263","Chapter of kohl not breaking the fasting person's fast (17). 1032 - Al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Zayd narrated, from Amr ibn Khalid, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1264","1033 - And from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I awaited the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to come out to us in Ramadan, and he came out from the house of Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) while she had applied kohl to him and filled his eyes (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with kohl."
"1265","Chapter of what is done by one who has intercourse or deliberately breaks fast (19). 1034 - Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman al-Sarraj, from Ata' and Amr ibn Shu'ayb, both said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am ruined. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What ruined you? He said: I fell upon my wife in Ramadan. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a donkey laden with dates, and he ordered some of it for him, saying: Take this and give it in charity. He said: O Messenger of Allah, between its two lava plains there is no household poorer than mine. He said: So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until his molars appeared, then said: Feed it to your family, and a day in place of a day, and seek Allah the Exalted's forgiveness. He said: I do not know whether in both their hadiths, or in one of their hadiths: a day in place of a day, and seek Allah the Exalted's forgiveness."
"1266","1035 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from al-Muttalib, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I broke fast one day in Ramadan. He said: Give charity for what you did, fast a day in its place, and seek Allah the Exalted's forgiveness."
"1267","1036 - Abu Ya'la said: Sahl ibn Zanjalah al-Razi narrated to us, al-Sabbah ibn Muharib narrated, from Harun ibn Antarah, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I broke fast one day in Ramadan. He said: Without excuse or travel? He said: Yes. He said: Evil is what you did. He said: What do you command me? He said: Free a neck. He said: By Him who sent you with the truth, I never owned a neck. He said: Fast two consecutive months. He said: I cannot do that. He said: Feed sixty poor persons. He said: By Him who sent you with the truth, I do not satiate my family. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a basket in it dates, and said: Give this in charity upon sixty poor persons. He said: To whom shall I give it? He said: To the poorest you know. He said: By Him who sent you with the truth, between its two lava plains there is no household needier than us. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give it in charity to your dependents."
"1268","Chapter of the concession to break fast while traveling and the validity of fasting for one who fasts in it (20). 1037/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Isma'il al-Talqani narrated to us, Jarir narrated, from Muslim, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) traveled in Ramadan, and fasted and broke fast."
"1269","1037/2 - He said: And Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated, from Muslim Abu Abdullah, from Mujahid with it."
"1270","1038 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, Abu Sa'id freedman of al-Mahri narrated to me who said: I came with a companion of mine from Umrah, and we sighted the crescent, the crescent of Ramadan, and we alighted in the land of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) on a day of intense heat, and we woke breaking fast except one man of us. Then Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) came upon us at midday and found our companion seeking the cool of the palms, and said: What is the matter with your companion? They said: Fasting. He said: What carried him to not break fast? Allah has granted him concession; had he died I would not have prayed over him. Halted, sound."
"1271","1039 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Rawh narrated to us, Sa'id narrated, from Qatadah, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Breaking fast while traveling is a firm duty. Halted, sound."
"1272","1040 - Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi said: Sulayman, that is Ibn Mu'adh, narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to fast while traveling and break fast."
"1273","1041 - Musaddad said: al-Mu'tamir narrated to us, al-Hakam ibn Aban narrated to me, from al-Ghitrif Abu Harun, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent two men on a need in Ramadan, and instructed one of them not to fast, and was silent about the other, so he fasted. When they came he said: What did you do? One of them said: I fasted, and the other said: I did not fast. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Both of you have done right. A good chain despite its being mursal."
"1274","1042 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Musa, I heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: We besieged Tustar with Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) over us, and he fasted and we fasted."
"1275","1043 - He said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) came to people besieging a fortress and ordered them to break fast."
"1276","1044 - He said: And Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of the Conquest of Makkah: Break fast, for it is a day of fighting."
"1277","1045 - He said: And Yahya narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Hajjaj, from Nafi', who said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) went out hastening lest fitnah fall in Madinah in Ramadan, and broke fast; when it was the night in which he enters, meaning Makkah, he woke fasting."
"1278","Chapter of the concession to break fast for the very old, the pregnant, and the nursing (21). 1046 - Abu Ya'la said: Ubaydullah ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, my father narrated, Imran narrated, from Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah, who said: Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) became too weak to fast, so he made a large bowl of tharid and invited thirty poor persons and fed them."
"1279","1047 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Azrah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The pregnant and nursing woman, when they fear, break fast and feed, with no makeup upon them. Its chain is good. Abu Dawud transmitted it from this route without his saying: with no makeup upon them."
"1280","Chapter of forbidding perpetual fasting and singling out a day or month (22). 1048 - Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Sufyan narrated, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Abu Ubaydah, from his mother (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: I never saw Abdullah fasting except the month of Ramadan, and two days. This is a sound chain."
"1281","1049/1 - Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Isa ibn Maymun, who said: I asked Ata' about a man who singles out a day to fast it. He said: Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to dislike singling out a month or a day to fast it, saying: The Messenger of Allah said: Whoever fasts perpetually, he has not fasted."
"1282","1049/2 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Hisham narrated, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he used to dislike fixing a day to fast it."
"1283","1049/3 - Yazid narrated to us, Hisham informed us similar to it, adding: And he used to dislike fasting Monday and Thursday."
"1284","1050 - Musaddad said: Bishr, that is Ibn al-Mufaddal, narrated, from Salamah, from al-Walid Abu Bishr, from Husayn ibn Abi al-Hurr, who said: I entered upon al-Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) on Friday while he was eating lunch, and he invited me, so I said: I am fasting. He said: Do not fast a day making its fast incumbent upon yourself."
"1285","1051 - Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me to single out Friday for fasting, and to cup while fasting - the hadith."
"1286","Chapter of suhur (23). 1052 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from his brother, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take suhur, for in suhur is blessing."
"1287","1053 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yahya, from Ulayy ibn Rabah, from Abu Qays, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take suhur, even with a grain of sand of dust."
"1288","1054 - He said: And Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah narrated, from Damrah and al-Muhajir sons of Habib, both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you is suhur, for it is the blessed breakfast; delay it as much as you can, and take suhur even with a sip of water."
"1289","1055 - He said: And Mu'awiyah ibn Yahya narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: Sometimes the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say to me: Bring near your blessed suhur; and sometimes there was nothing but two dates."
"1290","1056 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Bahr ibn Kaniz al-Saqqa narrated, from Imran al-Qasir, from Abu Sa'id al-Iskandarani, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Congregation is blessing, tharid is blessing, and suhur is blessing. Take suhur, for it increases strength, and it is of the Sunnah. Take suhur even with a sip of water - or upon a sip of water. Take suhur; Allah's blessings upon those who take suhur."
"1291","1057/1 - Abu Dawud said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Zuhayr ibn Abi Thabit al-A'ma, from Tamim ibn Iyad, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Alqamah ibn Ulathah was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) came to announce prayer to him, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gently, O Bilal, Alqamah is taking suhur. He said: While he was taking suhur with a head."
"1292","1057/2 - Abd ibn Humayd said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, Qays narrated, and he mentioned it with the wording: While the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taking suhur, when he finished his suhur Alqamah ibn Ulathah came, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for a head; while he was eating, Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) came, and he mentioned it."
"1293","1058/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, Hafs, that is Ibn Ghiyath, narrated, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from Abu Hubayrah, from his grandfather Shayban, who said: I came to the mosque and entered, leaning my back against the chamber of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taking suhur. So I cleared my throat, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Abu Yahya, come to breakfast. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I intend fasting. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And I intend fasting, but this mu'adhdhin of ours has poor sight, or something in his sight, for he called before dawn rose."
"1294","1058/2 - Al-Hasan ibn Sufyan said in his Musnad, and Mutayyan: Abu al-Sha'tha' Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, Hafs narrated with it."
"1295","1058/3 - Al-Hasan also said, and al-Baghawi: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us with it."
"1296","1058/4 - Ibn Abi Khaythamah narrated it from Asim ibn Ali, from Qays ibn al-Rabi', from Ash'ath with it. And he named Abu Hubayrah Yahya ibn Abbad."
"1297","1058/5 - Ibn Mandah said: al-Hasan ibn Mansur al-Imam informed us. 1058/6 - Ibn al-Sakan said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ta'i informed us, both said: al-Walid ibn Marwan informed us, my uncle Junadah ibn Marwan narrated, from his father, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Shayban, from his father, from his grandfather with it. 1058/7 - Ibn Mandah said: And Abd al-Rahman ibn Sharik followed him, from his father, from Ash'ath, meaning from his saying: from his father, from his grandfather. I (Ibn Hajar) say: The first is more likely correct."
"1298","1059 - Musaddad said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Shabib ibn Gharqadah, from Hibban ibn al-Harith, who said: We came to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was at the camp of Abu Musa, and found him eating. He said: Come near and eat. I said: I intend fasting. He said: And I intend fasting. Then he ate until when he finished he said to his mu'adhdhin Ibn al-Nabbah: Call the iqamah."
"1299","1060 - Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Muti' ibn Rashid narrated, from Tawbah, Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Look who is in the mosque and invite him. When it was Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), I invited them, and they ate, then went out, and he prayed Subh with them."
"1300","1061/1 - Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi said: Talhah ibn Amr narrated to us. 1061/2 - Abd ibn Humayd said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us. 1061/3 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us, both said: Talhah narrated, from Ata', from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We, the company of Prophets, were commanded to hasten our iftar and delay our suhur - the hadith. And it has already come in prayer."
"1301","Chapter of dislike of kissing for the fasting person and other than it, and what came of concession therein, with mention of kohl and siwak (24). 1062/1 - Ishaq said: I said to Abu Usamah: Did Umar ibn Hamzah narrate to you, Salim informed me, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a dream, and saw him not looking at me. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is my matter? He said: Are you not the one who kisses while fasting. He said: By Him who sent you with the truth as a Prophet, I shall not kiss after it while fasting. So he affirmed it and said: Yes."
"1302","1062/2 - Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Abu Usamah narrated, like it."
"1303","1063/1 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Marwan, that is Ibn Mu'awiyah, narrated to us, from Razin al-Bakri, our freedwoman called Salma of Bakr ibn Wa'il narrated to us, that she said: I heard A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon me and said: O A'ishah, is there a morsel; so I brought him a loaf, and he placed it upon his mouth, and said: O A'ishah, has anything of it entered my stomach; likewise is the kiss of the fasting person: breaking fast is only from what enters, not from what exits."
"1304","1063/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Mani' narrated to us with this."
"1305","1064 - Abu Ya'la also said: Abu Yasir al-Mustalmi narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Zayd brother of Hammad narrated, Amr ibn Khalid narrated, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ibn Umar, and from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us from the house of Hafsah (may Allah be pleased with her) having applied ithmid in Ramadan."
"1306","He also said: Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us, Hibban ibn Ali narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi' narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to apply kohl while he was fasting."
"1307","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Nu'man ibn al-Mundhir, from Ata, Tawus and Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used the miswak while he was fasting."
"1308","Ibn Abi Umar said: Marwan — that is Ibn Mu'awiyah — narrated to us, from Aban, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the kiss of a fasting person. He said: It is a sweet basil that he smells."
"1309","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id narrated to me, saying: A man asked Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: May I kiss my wife while I am fasting? He said: There is no harm. He said: Then may I kiss a woman other than her? He said: Uff (shame on you). He said: And I asked Sa'd ibn Malik, and he said: There is no harm."
"1310","Musaddad said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Layth, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast."
"1311","Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadham, from Yazid ibn Sa'id, the freed slave of Safiyyah, that he heard Safiyyah bint Huyayy, may Allah be pleased with her, saying: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast."
"1312","Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah — the nephew of al-Zuhri — narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped while he was fasting."
"1313","Al-Harith said: Ismail ibn Abi Ismail narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Mu'awiyah ibn Abd Allah ibn Tuwayli', from Aishah, the Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Everything of your wife is lawful for you while you are fasting except what is between the legs."
"1314","Chapter: The supplication being answered at the time of breaking the fast, and what the fasting person says at his breaking of the fast. 1073. Abu Dawud said: Abu Muhammad al-Mulayki narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The fasting person has, at his breaking of the fast, a supplication that is answered. And Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, whenever he broke his fast, would call his family and his children and supplicate. I say: This occurs in the Sunan of Ibn Majah with another chain from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, raised (to the Prophet) and halted, with another wording."
"1315","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Amr narrated to us, al-Sari ibn Khalid ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Ali, when you are fasting in the month of Ramadan, say after your breaking of the fast: O Allah, for You I fasted, upon You I relied, and upon Your provision I broke my fast; there will be written for you the like of the reward of those who fasted, without anything diminishing from their rewards."
"1316","Chapter: Whoever eats forgetfully has not broken his fast. 1075. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to me, saying: A man asked Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: I ate while I was fasting? He said: Nothing is upon you. He said: I drank while I was fasting? He said: Nothing is upon you. He said: Then I ate such-and-such while I was fasting? He said: O my son, you are not accustomed to fasting. Halted (mawquf), authentic chain."
"1317","Abd ibn Humayd said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Bashshar ibn Abd al-Malik, Umm Hakim bint Dinar, the freed slave woman of Umm Ishaq, narrated to me, from Umm Ishaq, who said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and bread and meat were brought. She said: I desired to eat from the food of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Come, O Umm Ishaq, and eat. She said: So I ate, then he handed me a bone with meat, so I raised it to my mouth, then I remembered that I was fasting, so my hand remained unable to raise it to my mouth nor to put it down. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is wrong with you, O Umm Ishaq? I said: O Messenger of Allah, I was fasting. He said: Complete your fast. Dhu al-Yadayn said: Now, after you have eaten your fill? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is only provision that Allah, the Exalted, drove to her. I say: Ahmad reported it, and in this there is an addition."
"1318","Chapter: Fasting Ashura. 1077. Abu Dawud said: Shu'bah narrated to us, Abu Ishaq informed me: I heard al-Aswad ibn Yazid saying: I never saw anyone who enjoined fasting Ashura more than Ali ibn Abi Talib and Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with them both. This chain is authentic."
"1319","Musaddad said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to fast Ashura, the tenth day."
"1320","Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, that he asked Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim about fasting Ashura, and he said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, would not fast it."
"1321","He said: Husayn ibn Numayr narrated to us, Abu Layla narrated to us, from Mazidah ibn Jabir, from his father, who said: I heard al-Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him, saying upon the pulpit of Kufah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to fast Ashura, so fast it."
"1322","Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Majza'ah ibn Zahir, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to fast the day of Ashura."
"1323","Abu Bakr said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from al-Hajari, from Abu Iyad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fast the day of Ashura, a day that the Prophets used to fast, so fast it."
"1324","Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq — that is Ibn Abi Isra'il — narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Jabir narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Yahya ibn Hubayrah, from Khabbab, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever among you has not eaten, let him fast; and whoever has eaten, let him complete the rest of his day. Abu Ya'la said: Meaning the day of Ashura."
"1325","Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-A'la ibn Hammad narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded fasting Ashura, but he himself would not fast it."
"1326","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, from Ulaylah bint Fulan al-Azdiyyah, my mother narrated to me, from Amat Allah, from Razinah, the maidservant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1327","Abu Ya'la said: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, Ulaylah narrated to us, from her mother, who said: I said to Amat Allah bint Razinah: O Amat Allah, did your mother Razinah narrate to you that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning the fast of Ashura? She said: Yes, he used to magnify it, to the point that he would call for his suckling infants and the suckling infants of his daughter Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, and blow (spittle) into their mouths and say: Do not nurse them until the night. Al-Harith did not mention the question."
"1328","Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Khalil, from Iyas ibn Harmalah, from Abu Qatadah, that a Bedouin asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about fasting the day of Arafah and the day of Ashura, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The day of Ashura expiates the year before it and the year after it, and the fast of Arafah expiates the year before it. I say: This chain is inverted, and the text is inverted; as for the chain, the correct is Harmalah ibn Iyas — thus Ahmad and others reported it. As for the text, the correct is that the day of Arafah is the one that expiates two years, and Ashura expiates one year; thus Muslim and others reported it from another route from Abu Qatadah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"1329","Chapter: Fasting Sha'ban and Shawwal. 1087. Al-Harith said: Abdullah ibn Awn narrated to us, Abu Ubaydah narrated to us, Abu Abd Allah narrated to us, Khalid ibn Ma'dan narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Murrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your Lord looks upon His creation on the night of the middle of Sha'ban, and forgives them all, except one who is a polytheist or one who is estranged (in enmity). They said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to fast Sha'ban, and Ramadan would enter while he was fasting, in veneration of Ramadan."
"1330","Abu Ya'la said: Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ibn Muhammad ibn Usamah, from his grandfather Usamah, may Allah be pleased with him, that whenever he broke his fast (after Ramadan), he would wake the next morning fasting from Shawwal, until completing it to its end."
"1331","Abu Ya'la said: Muhriz ibn Awn narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Layth, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sometimes join Sha'ban with Ramadan."
"1332","Chapter: The virtue of fasting the day of Arafah, except at Arafah. 1090/1. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Abu Hafs al-Ta'ifi, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts the day of Arafah will be forgiven two consecutive years. 1090/2. Al-Tabarani reported it, from Ubayd ibn Ghannam, from Abu Bakr; and from al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari and Abdan ibn Ahmad, who both said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Mu'awiyah narrated to us, the like of it."
"1333","1090/3. Abu Ya'la reported it, saying: Abu Bakr narrated to us."
"1334","1091. Abd ibn Humayd said: Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Farwah, from Iyad, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, raised (to the Prophet): Whoever fasts the day of Arafah will be forgiven two years, a year before it and a year after it. Ibn Majah reported it from this route, adding: from Abu Sa'id, from Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man. And Ishaq is very weak."
"1335","1092. Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Uthman ibn Hakim narrated to us, Nadabah narrated to me, saying: I heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: Whoever accompanies me, male or female, let him not fast the day of Arafah; for it is a day of eating, drinking, and remembering Allah, the Exalted."
"1336","1093. Musaddad said: al-Harith ibn Ubayd Abu Qudamah narrated to us, from Hud ibn Shihab ibn Abbad, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by dwellings at Arafat and said: Whose dwellings are these? We said: Those of Abd al-Qays. He spoke good to them and forbade them from fasting the day of Arafah. He said: And my father and Talq ibn Muhammad al-Khuza'i performed Hajj, and they differed about fasting the day of Arafah. My father said: Between you and me is Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib. They came to him and said: O Abu Muhammad, we differed about fasting the day of Arafah and have made you judge between us. He said: I shall inform you from one better than me, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he would not fast it."
"1337","1094. Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi said: Hawshab ibn Aqil narrated to us, from Mahdi al-Hajari, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting the day of Arafah at Arafah. The precise narrators differed from Hawshab, saying: from Mahdi, from Ikrimah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him; and from this route Abu Dawud, al-Nasa'i and Ibn Majah reported it."
"1338","Chapter: Prohibition of fasting the two days of Fitr and Adha. 1095. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Sa'id, from his grandfather, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting the day of Fitr and the day of Adha... the hadith."
"1339","Chapter: Prohibition of fasting the days of Tashriq. 1096/1. Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd, from his father, from his grandfather Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Stand and proclaim among the people that the days of Tashriq are days of eating and drinking; none shall fast in them."
"1340","1096/2. Al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Abu Ibrahim al-Madani informed us, from Ismail, the like of it."
"1341","1096/3. Ahmad ibn Mani' and al-Harith together said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, with it. Muhammad is weak, and he is Abu Ibrahim al-Madani whom al-Nadr gave the kunyah to."
"1342","1097/1. Al-Tayalisi said: al-Rabi' — that is Ibn Subayh — narrated to us, from Yazid — that is al-Raqashi — from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting six days of the year: three days of Tashriq, the day of Fitr, the day of Adha, and Friday alone among the days."
"1343","1097/2. Ahmad ibn Mani' and al-Harith together said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, al-Rabi' ibn Subayh and Marzuq Abu Abd Allah al-Shami both narrated to us, both from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting the three days of Tashriq after the day of sacrifice."
"1344","1097/3. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Rawh narrated to us, with this."
"1345","1097/4. He said: Musa ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Kahmas ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Yazid al-Raqashi, and he mentioned the like of the hadith of al-Tayalisi."
"1346","1097/5. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Khalid al-Tahhan narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Sa'id narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned it. I say: Muhammad ibn Khalid erred in it; it is rather Yazid al-Raqashi, not Qatadah."
"1347","1097/6. Al-Harith said: Abdullah ibn Awn narrated to us, Abu Ubaydah narrated to us, Abu Abd Allah narrated to us, from Yazid al-Raqashi, and he mentioned the like of the narration of al-Tayalisi."
"1348","1098/1. Musaddad said: Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us. 1098/2. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us. 1098/3. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Waki' narrated to us. 1098/4. Abd ibn Humayd said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us — all of them from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from al-Mundhir ibn Jahm, from Umar ibn Khaldah al-Ansari, from his mother, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, on the days of Tashriq to call out: O people, they are days of eating, drinking, and bi'al — meaning marriage. The wording is that of Abu Bakr."
"1349","1098/5. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, with this. Musa is weak."
"1350","1099. Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ishaq ibn Yahya, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl al-Hashimi, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded a man to call out on the days of Tashriq: These are days of eating, drinking, and marriage. I say: Amr ibn al-Husayn is weak."
"1351","Chapter: Prohibition of fasting the day of Friday. 1100. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay', from Qays ibn al-Sakan, who said: Some people from the companions of Abdullah came to Abu al-Darda', may Allah be pleased with him, on Friday while they were fasting, and he said: This is a day of festival, so I adjure you to break your fast."
"1352","1101. Musaddad said: Hafs narrated to us, from Layth, from Umayr ibn Abi Umayr, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was never seen not fasting on Friday, ever."
"1353","Chapter: Fasting three days of every month. 1102/1. Abu Bakr said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, Isra'il informed us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about fasting, but he was distracted from him, so Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Fast Ramadan and three days of every month. The man said: I seek refuge with Allah from you, O Abdullah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: And what do you seek? Fast Ramadan and three days of every month. This chain is good."
"1354","1102/2. Al-Bazzar reported it: Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us, Ubayd Allah narrated to us, with it. And he said: We do not know that Ibrahim narrated, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, except this."
"1355","1103. Musaddad said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Humayd al-A'raj, from Mujahid, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fasting Ramadan and three days of every month removes the waghar of the chest. They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the waghar of the chest? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Its pain and its rancor."
"1356","1104. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid narrated to us, Faraj ibn Fudalah narrated to us, Abu Hubayrah narrated to us, from Sadaqah, who said: A man asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about fasting, and he said: If you desire the fasting of the best of mankind — the unlettered, Arab, Qurashi Prophet, Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — then he used to fast three days of every month and say: It is the fasting of a lifetime."
"1357","1105/1. Al-Harith said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' narrated to us, Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Yazid ibn al-Hawtakiyyah, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked about the rabbit, and said: Who among you witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the Bedouin man came to him? A man from the people said: The Bedouin brought it, having seasoned and prepared it, and gifted it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: I saw it bleeding, i.e. menstruating. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the people: Eat. The Bedouin did not eat, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What prevented you from eating? He said: I am fasting. He said: Then why not the white days? Thus al-Hajjaj narrated it, and he is a mudallis. 1105/2. Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman and Hakim ibn Jubayr reported it, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, clarifying that the mentioned man who narrated this hadith is Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him. 1105/3. Abu Hanifah reported it from al-Haytham al-Sawwaf, from Musa, from Yazid ibn al-Hawtakiyyah, from Umar. And it was reported from Musa ibn Talhah, from Abu Hurayrah."
"1358","1106. Al-Harith said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, who said: I heard Musa ibn Salamah saying: I asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about fasting the three white days? He said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to fast them."
"1359","1107/1. Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-A'la ibn Hammad narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fasting the month of patience and three days of every month is the fasting of a lifetime, and it removes the wahar of the chest."
"1360","1107/2. Al-Bazzar said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, with it. And there is weakness in Hajjaj. 1107/3. But Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq followed him. Al-Bazzar also reported it."
"1361","1108. Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi said: Hammad ibn Yazid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah al-Muzani narrated to us, from Kahmas al-Hilali, that he narrated to him, mentioning a long hadith, and therein Kahmas said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of my Islam, then I was absent from him for a year, then I came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, do you recognize me? He said: Yes. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have not broken my fast since I parted from you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who commanded you to torture yourself? Fast one day of the month. I said: Increase me. He said: Fast two days. I said: Increase me. He said: Fast three days of every month."
"1362","Chapter: Specifying the three mentioned. 1109. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us, Shihab ibn Khirash narrated to us, from Salih ibn Jabalah, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever fasts Wednesday, Thursday and Friday, Allah, the Exalted, will build for him a house in Paradise whose outside is seen from its inside and whose inside is seen from its outside."
"1363","1110. Abu Ya'la said: Suwayd narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Abu Bakr, Muhammad ibn Zayd narrated to me, from Hanash al-San'ani, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it. And the hadith of Ibn Abbas and Ibn Umar will come."
"1364","1111. And from Baqiyyah, from Abu Bakr, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, with it."
"1365","Chapter: Fasting a day and breaking a day. 1112. Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, from Bishr ibn Harb, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Shall I fast a lifetime? He forbade him, and he repeated, and he forbade him, three times. But the fast of Dawud: fast a day and break a day; for that is the fast of Dawud, peace be upon him. That man continued fasting a day and breaking a day until he died."
"1366","1113. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid narrated to us, Faraj ibn Fudalah narrated to us, Abu Hubayrah narrated to us, from Sadaqah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: The best fasting is the fasting of my brother Dawud; he would fast half a lifetime, fasting a day and breaking a day."
"1367","1114. Abu Ya'la said: Shayban narrated to us, Abu Hilal narrated to us, Ghaylan ibn Jarir narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Ma'bad al-Zimmani narrated to me, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he came upon a man, and they said: He has not broken his fast for such-and-such. He said: He neither fasted nor broke fast. When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saw his anger, he said: O Messenger of Allah, fasting two days and breaking a day? He said: Can anyone bear that? He said: O Messenger of Allah, fasting a day and breaking a day? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is the fast of my brother Dawud. He said: O Messenger of Allah, fasting a day and breaking two days? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And who can bear that? He said: O Messenger of Allah, fasting Monday? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is a day on which I was born, and a day on which prophethood was sent down upon me. He said: O Messenger of Allah, fasting Arafah and Ashura? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One of them expiates a year, and the other expiates what is before it and what is after it. I say: What is preserved with this chain is: from Abdullah ibn Ma'bad, from Abu Qatadah, in full; Muslim and the authors of the Sunan reported it from that route."
"1368","Chapter: Laylat al-Qadr. 1115/1. Ishaq said: al-Mughirah ibn Salamah al-Makhzumi informed us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from my maternal uncle al-Falatan ibn Asim al-Jarmi, who said: We were sitting waiting for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he came to us with anger on his face until he sat, then we saw his face brightening, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Laylat al-Qadr and the Messiah of misguidance were made clear to me, so I came out to clarify them for you, but I met at the entrance of the mosque two men quarreling — or he said: fighting — with Satan among them, so I separated them and was made to forget them; I shall chant for you from them a chant: as for Laylat al-Qadr, seek it in the last ten, on the odd nights; as for the Messiah of misguidance, he is a man with a receding forehead, a wiped eye, a broad chest, as if he is so-and-so son of Abd al-Uzza, or Abd al-Uzza ibn Qatan."
"1369","1115/2. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from his maternal uncle al-Falatan ibn Asim, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I saw Laylat al-Qadr, then I was made to forget it; and I saw the Messiah of misguidance, and I saw two men quarreling, so I separated them and was made to forget them. As for Laylat al-Qadr, seek it in the last ten; as for the Messiah of misguidance, he is a man with a receding forehead, a wiped left eye, a broad chest, with blood in it, as if he is so-and-so son of Abd al-Uzza, or Abd al-Uzza son of so-and-so."
"1370","1115/3. Husayn ibn Ali narrated to us, from Za'idah, from Asim, from his father, from al-Falatan, abridged: Whoever among you is seeking Laylat al-Qadr, let him seek it in the last ten."
"1371","1116. My father said: So I narrated it to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he said: What amazed you of that? Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when he would invite the elders from the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), would invite me with them, saying: Do not speak until they speak. One day — or one night — he invited us and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about Laylat al-Qadr what you have known: Seek it in the last ten, on the odd nights. Which odd is it? A man spoke by his opinion: ninth, seventh, fifth, third. He said to me: Why do you not speak, O Ibn Abbas? I said: O Commander of the Believers, if you wish I shall speak. He said: I only invited you to speak. He said: I only speak by my opinion. He said: It is your opinion that I ask. I said: I heard Allah, the Exalted, mention seven often: He mentioned the heavens as seven, and the earths as seven, until he said — among what he said — and what the earth grows as seven. He said: All you have said I knew except this; what do you mean by your saying: and what the earth grows as seven? He said: Allah, the Exalted, says: Then We split the earth in clefts, and caused to grow therein grain, and grapes and greens, and olives and palms, and dense gardens, and fruits and herbage — a provision for you. The gardens are every entwined thing is a garden, and the herbage (abb) is what the earth grows of what people do not eat. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Are you unable to say the like of what this boy said, whose head has not yet evened? Then he said to me: I had forbidden you to speak with them, but when I invite you, speak with them. I say: Ahmad reported the raised hadith of Umar alone."
"1372","1117/1. Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Abu Zumayl Simak al-Hanafi, Malik ibn Marthad narrated to us, from his father, who said: I said to Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning Laylat al-Qadr? He said: Yes. I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me about Laylat al-Qadr: is it in Ramadan or in other than Ramadan? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather in Ramadan. I said: O Messenger of Allah, is it with the Prophets while they exist, then when the Prophets are taken it is raised, or is it until the Day of Resurrection? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, rather it is until the Day of Resurrection. I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me in which Ramadan it is? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In the last ten; do not ask me about anything after it. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I adjure you by my right: in which of the ten is it? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry with me with an anger the like of which he had never been angry with me before nor after, and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Had Allah willed He would have informed you of it; seek it in the last seven; do not ask me about anything after it."
"1373","1117/2. Ishaq said: Abu Dawud al-Hafari informed us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Marthad or Ibn Marthad, from his father, who said: I was with Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, and he was asked about Laylat al-Qadr, and he mentioned the like of it up to his saying: the last ten, and did not mention what is after it. I say: This chain is good, authentic. Ahmad and al-Nasa'i also reported it from the hadith of Abu Zumayl, but not in full."
"1374","1117/3. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Waki' narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Marthad ibn Abi Marthad, from his father, who said: I was with Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned it, adding therein: I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform us of it? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I been permitted therein I would have informed you of it."
"1375","1117/4. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ikrimah ibn Ammar, Abu Zumayl Simak al-Hanafi narrated to me, Malik ibn Marthad narrated to us, my father Marthad narrated to me, saying: I asked Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the like of it."
"1376","1118/1. Ishaq said: Abdah ibn Sulayman informed us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Abu Hazim, the freed slave of Hudhayl, who said: I stayed in retreat in the mosque of Madinah with a man from the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Banu Bayadah, in the last ten of Ramadan, in a tent of his screened at its door with a piece of mat. He said: While we were in the mosque and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in a tent of his, he lifted the mat from the door and signaled to those in the mosque: Gather. So we gathered, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) admonished us with an admonition the like of which I never heard from any admonisher, saying: When one of you stands praying, he converses with his Lord, blessed and exalted; let him consider with what he converses, and let none of you raise his voice over others with the Quran. Then he let down the mat, and each of us returned to his place. Some of us said to others: This night has a matter; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) admonished us in it — and behold it was the night of twenty-three. I say: Ishaq made it from the Musnad of Abu Hazim, freed slave of Banu Hudhayl. 1118/2. Al-Nasa'i reported it in al-I'tikaf from routes, most of them from the narration of Abu Hazim from al-Bayadi. 1118/3. Imam Ahmad reported the story of the prohibition of raising the voice in recitation from the route of Malik, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Hazim, from al-Bayadi. They differed about this Abu Hazim: in most narrations he is the freed slave of Banu Ghifar, named Dinar; in this narration he is the freed slave of Banu Hudhayl. And Allah knows best."
"1377","1119. Musaddad said: Abdullah — that is Ibn Dawud — narrated to us, from Fitr, from Abdullah ibn Sharik, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah and Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Laylat al-Qadr is the night of twenty-seven."
"1378","1120. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Husayn narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Hawt, from Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Laylat al-Qadr is the night of seventeen: the day of Furqan, the day the two hosts met; I do not doubt, nor do I make exception."
"1379","1121. Al-Harith said: Kathir ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Burqan narrated to us, saying: I heard a man from Quraysh saying: Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say: It is the night in whose day the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met the people of Badr. He said: Allah, mighty and majestic, says: And what We sent down upon Our servant on the day of Furqan, the day the two hosts met. Ja'far said: It reached me that it is the night of sixteen or seventeen."
"1380","1122. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Hudhayfah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I saw the moon on Laylat al-Qadr as if it were half a bowl."
"1381","1123. He also said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from al-A'mash: I was informed from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out one night intending to inform us of Laylat al-Qadr, of which we had been informed; he heard noise in the mosque, so it was snatched from him."
"1382","1124. He said: Abu al-Walid al-Qurashi narrated to us, al-Walid — that is Ibn Muslim — narrated to us, Salim informed me that he heard Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Uthman narrating from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Al-Juhani said: O Messenger of Allah, we are absent, as you know, and cannot attend this month; inform us of Laylat al-Qadr? I said: O Messenger of Allah, this is the night of twenty-three, with eight (nights) remaining? He said: Nay, this month decreases; it is with seven remaining."
"1383","Chapter: I'tikaf. 1125. Al-Harith said: Ashhal narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad, who said: A man asked Shurayh about a woman who vowed to observe i'tikaf in Rajab of that year in the mosque — while Ziyad, or Ibn Ziyad, had forbidden women to observe i'tikaf in the mosque. Shurayh said: I do not say that it is in a revealed book, nor in a past Sunnah; it is only an opinion: she fasts Rajab of that year, then when she breaks her fast, a poor person breaks with her every day, or she feeds a poor person every night — two rites for one rite; Allah does what He wills. This chain is authentic, halted upon Shurayh."
"1384","1126. Ishaq said: al-Nadr — that is Ibn Shumayl — informed us, Hammad — that is Ibn Salamah — narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to me that Safiyyah observed i'tikaf, then some of her family fell ill, so she sought permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to visit him, and he said: Hold the two doorposts and do not enter. This is mursal, or mu'dal; its men are trustworthy."
"1385","Book of Hajj. Chapter: The beginning of the obligation of Hajj. 1127. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Jarir narrated to us, from Qabus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When Ibrahim, peace be upon him, finished building the House, he was told: Proclaim Hajj among the people. He said: How far will my voice reach? It was said: Proclaim, and upon Me is conveyance. So Ibrahim called out: O people, Hajj to the Ancient House has been decreed upon you. Whoever was between heaven and earth heard him. Do you not see that people perform Hajj from the horizons of the earth, chanting labbayk."
"1386","Chapter: Command to hasten Hajj. 1128. Al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, Husayn ibn Umar narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Perform Hajj, for I see as if an Abyssinian, bald, with a pickaxe in his hand, demolishing it stone by stone. We said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: By your opinion? He said: No, by the One Who split the seed and created the souls; rather I heard it from your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1387","1129. Abu Bakr and Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Jarir narrated to us, Mansur narrated to us, from Kilab ibn Ali, from Mansur ibn Sulayman, from the nephew of Jubayr ibn Mut'im, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sarurah in Islam."
"1388","Chapter: Virtue of one who looks after the pilgrim in his family with good. 1130. Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, mentioning the hadith, and therein: Whoever looks after a pilgrim or an umrah performer in his family with good will have the like of his reward, complete, without anything diminishing from his reward."
"1389","Chapter: Virtue of the pilgrim. 1131. Musaddad said: Attaf ibn Khalid narrated to us, Ismail ibn Rafi' narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, the companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque of al-Khayf, when a man from the Ansar and a man from Thaqif came to him; when they greeted, they said: We came to you, O Messenger of Allah, to ask you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you wish, I shall inform you of what you ask me about, and if you wish that I be silent and you ask, I shall do so. They said: Inform us, O Messenger of Allah, that we may increase in faith — or increase in certainty; Ismail doubted. The Ansari said to the Thaqafi: Ask. He said: Rather you ask, for I recognize your right, so ask. The Ansari said: Inform us, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You came to ask me about your leaving your house heading for the Sacred House and what is for you therein; about your tawaf of the House and what is for you therein; about your two rak'ahs after tawaf and what is for you therein; about your tawaf between al-Safa and al-Marwah and what is for you therein; about your standing at Arafah and what is for you therein; about your stoning the Jamarat and what is for you therein; about your sacrifice and what is for you therein; about your shaving your head and what is for you therein; and about your tawaf after that and what is for you therein. He said: By the One Who sent you with the truth, it is for this that I came to ask you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you leave your house heading for the Sacred House, your camel does not place a hoof nor lift it but Allah, the Exalted, writes for you thereby a good deed, erases from you thereby a sin, and raises for you thereby a degree. As for your tawaf of the House, as for your two rak'ahs after tawaf, they are like freeing a slave from the children of Ismail; as for your tawaf between al-Safa and al-Marwah, it is like freeing seventy slaves; as for your standing on the evening of Arafah, Allah, the Exalted, descends to the lowest heaven and boasts of you to the angels, saying: These are My servants; they came to Me disheveled and dusty from every deep pass, hoping for My mercy and My forgiveness; had your sins been the number of sand or the foam of the sea, I would have forgiven them. Disperse, My servants, forgiven for you and for whom you interceded. As for your stoning the Jamarat, for every pebble you threw is (expiation for) a major, destructive, obligating sin; as for your sacrifice, it is stored for you with your Lord; as for your shaving your head, for every hair you shaved is a good deed, and a sin is erased from you thereby. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what if the sins are less than that? He said: Then it will be stored for you in your good deeds. As for your tawaf of the House after that, you circumambulate with no sin upon you; an angel comes until he places his hand between your shoulders, then says: Act for the future, for what is past has been forgiven for you. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. It already preceded in prayer."
"1390","1132. Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, mentioning a long hadith, and therein: Whoever goes out as pilgrim or umrah performer, for every step until he returns is a million million good deeds, the erasure of a million million evil deeds, and the raising of a million million degrees; for him with his Lord, for every dirham he spends is a million million dirhams, and for every dinar a million million dinars, and for every good deed he does a million million good deeds, until he returns; he is in the guarantee of Allah, the Exalted: if He takes his soul He admits him to Paradise, and if He returns him He returns him forgiven and answered. So seize his supplication when he arrives, before he incurs sins; for he intercedes for a hundred thousand men on the Day of Resurrection. This hadith is fabricated."
"1391","Chapter: The sanctity of Makkah. 1133. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Ubaydah ibn Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, Malik ibn Su'ayr narrated to us, Furat ibn al-Ahnaf narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he stood in a door leading into the mosque, the mosque of Mina, praised Allah, the Exalted, and extolled Him, then said: These are but the slaves of the disbelievers, the wicked; they have found it hard to come every year, stealing our wealth and binding our captives; Allah, the Exalted, has made their blood and wealth lawful by what they made lawful of our blood and wealth — meaning Najdah al-Khariji and his companions. I sent to them and they were given what was asked; these are the captives and these are the mounts — distinguish them; whatever you recognize, take it. But I do not consider it right that blood be shed in the sanctuary of Allah, the Exalted. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the Farewell Hajj: Which land is most sacred? It was said: Makkah. He said: Which month is most sacred? It was said: Dhu al-Hijjah. He said: Which day is most sacred? It was said: The day of the Greater Hajj. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your blood and your wealth are sacred to you until you meet your Lord, like the sacredness of this day of yours, in this land of yours, in this month of yours."
"1392","1134/1. Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, Sadaqah ibn Yasar narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed, mentioning the hadith, saying: O people, time has turned, and today it is as it was on the day Allah created the heavens and the earth; the number of months with Allah is twelve months in the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic — of them four are sacred: Rajab of Mudar between Jumada and Sha'ban, Dhu al-Qa'dah, Dhu al-Hijjah, and al-Muharram; postponement (nasi') is an increase in disbelief by which those who disbelieved are led astray, to: what Allah has forbidden. That is because they would make Safar lawful one year and unlawful one year, and likewise al-Muharram; that postponement is from Satan — the hadith... And therein: Which day is this? They said: A sacred day. He said: Which month is this? They said: A sacred month? He said: Which land is this? They said: A sacred land. He said: Allah, the Exalted, has forbidden to you your blood, your wealth, and your honors, like the sacredness of this day in this month. Verily there is no prophet after me, nor any nation after you. Let your present convey to your absent. Then he raised his hands and said: O Allah, bear witness; O Allah, bear witness — three times. It will come, Allah willing, in the chapter of the prohibition of blood in the Book of Hudud, with the remainder of the routes of this hadith, some in the chapter of the sermon at Mina."
"1393","1134/2. Abd ibn Humayd said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, and mentioned it in full. 1134/3. Abu Ya'la reported it from another route, from Musa."
"1394","Chapter: Virtue of Hajj on foot. 1135/1. Abu Ya'la said: Mujahid ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sulaym al-Ta'ifi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi narrated to us, from one who narrated to him, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said to his sons: Go out obedient from Makkah on foot, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The mounted pilgrim has, for every step his mount takes, seventy good deeds; the walking one has, for every step he takes, seven hundred good deeds from the good deeds of the Sanctuary. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what are the good deeds of the Sanctuary? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A good deed is a hundred thousand. 1135/2. Isa ibn Sawadah followed him, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Zadham, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both. Ibn Khuzaymah and al-Hakim reported it from his route; al-Bayhaqi said: Isa is unknown."
"1395","Chapter: Virtue of following Hajj with Umrah. 1136. Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Abbad narrated to us from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Follow Hajj with Umrah; by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, they remove poverty and sins as the bellows remove the dross of iron."
"1396","1137. Al-Harith said: Hawdhah narrated to us, Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from a son of Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Follow Hajj with Umrah — the hadith."
"1397","Chapter: Riding the sea for Hajj. 1138. Al-Harith said: al-Khalil ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, Habib ibn al-Shahid narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None rides the sea except a warrior, a pilgrim, or an umrah performer."
"1398","Chapter: Encouragement to Hajj every five years. 1139/1. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from al-Ala' ibn al-Musayyib, from his father, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, mighty and majestic, says: A servant whose body I have kept sound and whose livelihood I have widened, and five years pass over him without coming to Me as a visitor, is deprived."
"1399","1139/2. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, with this. 1139/3. Abd al-Razzaq said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed us, from al-Ala' ibn al-Musayyib, with it. I say: They differed over al-Ala' in it."
"1400","1140. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, Abu Sa'id narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Khabbab, from a man, from Khabbab ibn al-Aratt, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, mighty and majestic, says: A servant whose body I have kept sound and whose provision I have widened, and five pilgrimages pass over him without coming to Me therein, is deprived."
"1401","Chapter: Command to make children and slaves perform Hajj, and its obligation upon them when they come of age. 1141. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Salim ibn Hayyan, Musa ibn Qatan narrated to me, from Aminah bint Muhriz, who said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Make the offspring perform Hajj; do not consume their provisions and leave their sins upon their necks."
"1402","1142. Al-Humaydi and Ibn Abi Umar together said: Sufyan narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, the brother of Musa ibn Uqbah, narrated to me, mentioning a hadith about the Hajj of a child. He said: So I narrated it to Ibn al-Munkadir, and he performed Hajj with all his family."
"1403","1143. Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, al-Munkadir ibn Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir informed me, from his father, that it was said to him: Do you take children for Hajj? He said: Yes, I present them to Allah, the Exalted."
"1404","1144. Al-Harith said: Ismail ibn Abi Ismail narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Uthman, from Abu Atiq, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If a slave performs ten pilgrimages, an (obligatory) Hajj remains upon him if he finds a way to it; if a Bedouin performs ten pilgrimages, a Hajj remains upon him when he migrates, if he finds a way to it; if a child performs ten pilgrimages, the Hajj of Islam remains upon him when he attains understanding, if he finds a way to it."
"1405","Chapter: Dislike of Hajj upon dung-eating camels. 1145. Musaddad said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, from his father: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came to Makkah, and was informed that a freed slave of Amr ibn al-As had dung-eating camels; he sent for them and expelled them from Makkah, saying: Camels upon which firewood is carried and water transported. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Neither Hajj nor Umrah shall be performed upon them. Its chain is authentic, halted (mawquf)."
"1406","1146. Musaddad said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Riding dung-eating animals was forbidden."
"1407","Chapter: Carrying upon a mount in Hajj counts from the path of Allah. 1147. Abu Bakr said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Ismail, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ma'qil ibn Abi Ma'qil: that his mother came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Ma'qil had promised me that he would not perform Hajj except with me; he performed Hajj upon his mount, and I could not walk; I asked him for the dates of a palm tree, and he said: It is the food of his dependents; I asked him for a young camel of his, and he said: It is in the path of Allah and I shall not give it to you. He said: O Abu Ma'qil, what does Umm Ma'qil say? He said: She spoke truth. He said: Give her your young camel; Hajj is from the path of Allah, the Exalted. So he gave her his young camel."
"1408","1148. Abu Ya'la said: Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Abd al-Rahim — that is Ibn Sulayman — narrated to us, from al-Mukhtar ibn Fulful, from Talq ibn Habib, from Abu Taliq, who said: His wife said to him, while he had a camel and a she-camel: Give me your camel that I may perform Hajj upon it. He said: It is confined in the path of Allah, the Exalted. She said: It is in the path of Allah that I perform Hajj upon it. He refused. She said: Then give me the she-camel, and perform Hajj upon your camel? He said: I do not prefer anyone over myself. She said: Then give me from your provision. He said: I have nothing extra beyond what I go out with; had I, I would have given you. She said: Since you have done what you did, convey my greeting to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when you meet him, and tell him what I said to you. When he met the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he conveyed her greeting and informed him of what she said to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Umm Taliq spoke truth; had you given her your camel it would have been in the path of Allah; had you given her your she-camel it would have been in the path of Allah; had you given her from your provision Allah would have replaced it for you. I said: O Prophet of Allah, what equals Hajj? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: An Umrah in Ramadan."
"1409","Chapter: Validity of the Hajj of camel drivers. 1149. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Ata said: A man asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: I hire myself to these people and perform the rites with them; is there reward for me? He said: Yes: Those, for them is a share of what they earned — the verse."
"1410","1150. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Shabib, Abu al-Salil narrated to us: I said to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: I have mounts that I rent out in Hajj, and I strive upon my dependents; some people claimed I have no Hajj, because they are rented. He said: They lied; for you is reward in your Hajj and reward in your striving upon your dependents — two rewards for you. Abu Dawud reported it from another route with other than this wording."
"1411","Chapter: Hajj on behalf of another. 1151. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Hatim al-Tawil narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man chanting labbayk on behalf of Shubrumah, and said: What is Shubrumah? He mentioned a relative of his. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you perform Hajj for yourself? He said: No. He said: Perform Hajj for yourself, then perform Hajj for Shubrumah."
"1412","1152. Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Ata, and mentioned it mursal. What is preserved in this hadith is Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"1413","1153. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: A man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): My father cannot perform Hajj except lying on his side; shall I perform Hajj for him? He said: Yes. He said: My mother died without bequest; shall I give charity for her? He said: Yes."
"1414","1154. Al-Harith said: Ishaq ibn Bishr narrated to us, Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, the Exalted, admits by a single Hajj three persons into Paradise: the deceased, the pilgrim, and the one who carries it out."
"1415","1155. Al-Harith said: Ismail ibn Abi Ismail narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Shu'ayb al-Madani, raising it, the like of it."
"1416","Chapter: The spatial miqats. 1156. Ishaq said: Hafs — that is Ibn Ghiyath — narrated to us, from Hajjaj — that is Ibn Artah — from Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed Dhat Irq for the people of Iraq. Hajjaj is weak."
"1417","1157. Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn Qusayt narrated to us, from his father Yazid, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Ubayy, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram from the mosque of Dhu al-Hulayfah."
"1418","1158. Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Yahya ibn Sirin, that he performed Hajj with Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, and he narrated to him that he entered ihram from al-Aqiq."
"1419","Chapter: Dislike of entering ihram from other than the miqat. 1159. Musaddad said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, who said: Al-Hasan narrated to them that Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him — I think he said — meaning he entered ihram from Basrah; when he came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and it had reached him, he was harsh with him, saying: People will say that a man from the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram from a city among the cities."
"1420","Chapter: The temporal miqats. 1160. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Asad ibn Amr narrated to us, al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: It is from the Sunnah that one not enter ihram for Hajj except in the months of Hajj."
"1421","Chapter: Virtue of the muhrim. 1161. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, from Fulan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: No man lays down his garment while in ihram and the sun strikes him until it sets, but it sets with his sins."
"1422","1162/1. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, from his brother Abdullah ibn Ubaydah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever completes his rites, and the Muslims are safe from his tongue and hand, will be forgiven what preceded of his sin and what follows."
"1423","1162/2. Abd ibn Humayd said: Abdullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, with it, but did not say: and what follows."
"1424","1163/1. Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The delegation of Allah, the Exalted, are three: the pilgrim, the umrah performer, and the warrior. Muhammad is weak."
"1425","1163/2. Al-Bazzar said: From al-Walid ibn Amr ibn Sukayn, Abu Asim narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, with it. And he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd alone narrated it, and he has hadiths not followed upon."
"1426","1164. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, Mirdas ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, saying: I entered upon Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he narrated to us, saying: There is no one who chants talbiyah but Allah, the Exalted, says: Rejoice. The uncle of Mirdas said: O Abu Muhammad, by Allah, Allah, the Exalted, gives glad tidings only of Paradise. He said: Who are you, O son of my brother? He said: I am Mirdas ibn Shaddad al-Junaydi. He said: O son of my brother, our best used to follow one another upon that."
"1427","1165. Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Suqah narrated to us, saying: It was said to Ibn al-Munkadir: Do you perform Hajj while debt is upon you? He said: Hajj settles debt sooner."
"1428","1166. Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Tirkhan, from Abdullah ibn Tawus, who said: My father, when we headed to Makkah, would travel with us a month from his place; when returning, he would travel with us two months. That was mentioned to him, and he said: It reached me that a man remains in the path of Hajj until he enters among his family."
"1429","1167. Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This House is the pillar of Islam; whoever goes out heading for this House, as pilgrim, umrah performer, or visitor, is guaranteed by Allah: if He takes his soul, He admits him to Paradise; if He returns him, He returns him with gain and reward."
"1430","1168. Al-Harith said: Ishaq ibn Bishr narrated to us, Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies on the road to Makkah, Allah, blessed and exalted, will neither present him on the Day of Resurrection nor reckon him."
"1431","1169. Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn Hammad narrated to us, Husayn — meaning al-Ju'fi — narrated to us, from Ibn al-Sammak, from A'idh, from Ata, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever goes out from this direction for Hajj or Umrah and dies therein will neither be presented nor reckoned; it will be said to him: Enter Paradise. This A'idh is Ibn Nusayr — with nun and unpointed letter, diminutive — weak; Ibn al-Sammak is Muhammad ibn Subayh, in him is weakness."
"1432","1170. Abu Ya'la also said: Ibrahim ibn Ziyad Sabalan narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Jamil ibn Abi Maymunah, from Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever goes out as pilgrim and dies, the reward of the pilgrim will be written for him until the Day of Resurrection; whoever goes out as umrah performer and dies, the reward of the umrah performer will be written for him until the Day of Resurrection; whoever goes out as warrior in the path of Allah and dies, the reward of the warrior will be written for him until the Day of Resurrection."
"1433","Chapter: Supplication of the pilgrim and umrah performer. 1171. Abu Ya'la said: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Samad narrated to us, Qasim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Asim, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came seeking permission for Umrah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O my brother, supplicate, and do not forget us in righteous supplication. Abu Dawud reported it from the hadith of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, from himself."
"1434","Chapter: Changing Hajj into Umrah and vice versa, and what came about qiran. 1172. Ishaq said: al-Mughirah ibn Salamah al-Makhzumi informed us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Ishaq, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him: that he was asked about mut'ah in Hajj, and said: It was for us, not for you."
"1435","1173. Abu Ya'la said: Sufyan — that is Ibn Waki' — narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Humayd, from Abu Malih, Ma'qil ibn Yasar, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, who said: I performed Hajj with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he found Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, removing her clothes, and said: What is wrong with you? She said: I was informed that you released your family from ihram. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, whoever has no sacrificial animal with him; as for us, we have not exited, for we have sacrificial camels with us, until we reach Arafat."
"1436","1174. Musaddad said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, who said: Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, arrived with companions of his having joined Hajj and Umrah; it was said to Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him: Imran has arrived with companions of his with Hajj and Umrah. He sent to him: Choose one of them. Imran said: The Commander of the Believers forbade us, yet he gave us choice; I choose Hajj."
"1437","1175. He said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, Uthman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Sa'd, freed slave of al-Hasan ibn Ali, who said: We went out with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, until when we were at Dhu al-Hulayfah, he said: I intend to join Hajj and Umrah; whoever of you desires that, let him say as I say. Then he chanted labbayk, saying: With an Umrah and a Hajj together. Authentic, halted (mawquf)."
"1438","1176. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and Ahmad ibn Mani' together said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Kilab ibn Ali, from Mansur ibn Sulayman, from the nephew of Jubayr ibn Mut'im, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon al-Marwah with a mishqas in his hand, shortening his hair with it, saying: Umrah has entered into Hajj until the Day of Resurrection."
"1439","Chapter: What suffices the qarin of tawaf and sa'y. 1177/1. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ya'la ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from his father, from Ghaylan ibn Jami', from Layth, from Ata, Tawus and Mujahid, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions performed only one tawaf for their Hajj and Umrah."
"1440","1177/2. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Yahya ibn Ya'la narrated to us, with it."
"1441","1177/3. Abu Ya'la also said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, with it. I say: Layth is weak. The hadith of Jabir is with Muslim from another route; the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, is in the Sunan."
"1442","Chapter: Tamattu'. 1178. Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Hisham narrated to us, from al-Hasan, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, intended to forbid mut'at al-Hajj, so Ubayy ibn Ka'b stood to him and said: That is not for you; the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic, came down with it, and we performed Umrah with it with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, left it."
"1443","1179. Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, saying: Ma'mar said, Ibn Tawus said, from his father: My father and Abu Musa stood to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Will you not inform the people of the matter of this mut'ah? He said: Has anyone remained but practiced it? As for me, I practice it."
"1444","1180. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Had I performed Umrah, then Umrah, then Hajj, I would have performed tamattu'."
"1445","1181. He said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, Nafi' informed me, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If they separate Hajj and Umrah, Umrah in other than the months of Hajj is more complete for the Hajj and Umrah of one of you."
"1446","1182. Musaddad said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', who said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, when he entered ihram from Makkah, would not perform sa'y until returning from Mina."
"1447","1183. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Hisham, from Hafsah, who said: We entered ihram with Umrah in Ramadan, arrived at Makkah in Shawwal while people were then numerous, and we asked; everyone we asked said: It is mut'ah."
"1448","1184. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, who said: Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Perform Hajj alone, and leave the saying of this blind man of yours. Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The one whose heart Allah blinded is rather you; ask your mother about this."
"1449","1185. Abu Ya'la said: Hajjaj ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Hakim narrated to me, Zam'ah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah informed me, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him: that he performed tamattu' of Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Allah knows best."
"1450","Chapter: Permissibility of performing Umrah before Hajj. 1186. Ishaq said: Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri' informed us, Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, I heard Yazid ibn Abi Habib saying: Abu Imran al-Tujibi narrated to me that he performed Hajj with his freedmen. He said: I met Umm Salamah, the Mother of the Believers, and said to her: I have never performed Hajj; with which shall I begin, Hajj or Umrah? She said: Begin with whichever you wish. I said to her: People say if one has never performed Hajj, let him begin with Hajj. She said to me: Begin with whichever you wish. I came to Safiyyah and asked her, and she said to me the like of what Umm Salamah said: Begin with whichever you wish. Then I came to Umm Salamah and informed her of Safiyyah's saying, and Umm Salamah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O family of Muhammad, whoever of you performs Hajj, let him make an Umrah with his Hajj, or with a Hajj."
"1451","Chapter of what the muhrim must avoid (26). 1187. Musaddad said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Aslam the freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab saw two dyed garments on Talhah while he was in ihram, and said: What is this? He said: O Commander of the Faithful, there is no harm in it, it is only mishq (red dye). He said: You, O group, are leaders; people follow you, and perhaps an ignorant person if he saw you would say: I saw two dyed garments on Talhah, so he would wear dyed clothes in ihram. Let me never come to know that any of you wears a dyed garment in ihram. This is a sahih mawquf isnad, and it is a basis for blocking means (sadd al-dhara'i')."
"1452","1188. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr, from Abu Ja'far who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) saw two dyed garments on Abdullah ibn Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with them both) while he was in ihram, and said: What is this? So Ali said: I do not think anyone teaches us the Sunnah."
"1453","1189. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd who said: A woman asked Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both): Shall I wash my clothes while I am in ihram? He said: Allah the Exalted has no need of your dirt."
"1454","1190. He said: And Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The muhrim may bathe, and may wash his clothes if he wishes. This is sahih mawquf."
"1455","1191. He said: And Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), the like of it."
"1456","1192. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, that he heard Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The woman in ihram (muhillah) shall not wear perfumed clothes, but she may wear safflower-dyed clothes; and I do not consider yellowness to be perfume. This is sahih mawquf."
"1457","1193. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid; my sister narrated to me that she saw A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) on the evening of al-Tarwiyah (8th of Dhu al-Hijjah) wearing a rose-colored shirt (dir') and a black headcover while she was in ihram."
"1458","1194. He said: And Khalid narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated, from his sister and his mother, that they both entered upon A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) while she was wearing a rose-colored shirt and a black headcover. It was said to her: Does the muhrimah cover her face? So she lifted her headcover like this, from her chest toward her head, and said: There is no harm in this."
"1459","1195. Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated, from al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): that she permitted the muhrimah to wear gloves."
"1460","1196. And he said: al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, Hammam narrated, from Ata, from Ya'la ibn Umayyah, from his father who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing a cloak (jubbah) with traces of khaluq (perfume) on him, and said: What shall I do regarding my umrah? Then revelation descended and he (the Prophet) was screened with a garment. And Umayyah loved to see him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when revelation had descended upon him, the hadith. I (Ibn Hajar) say: al-Abbas erred in it; it is rather from Ibn Ya'la ibn Umayyah, from his father; and the hadith belongs to Ya'la, not from the hadith of his father Umayyah."
"1461","1197. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Arubah, from Matar al-Warraq, from al-Hasan, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Any man who marries while in ihram, we shall take his wife away from him, and his marriage is not valid."
"1462","1198. Al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Isra'il narrated, from Abu Abdillah who said: I was with Sa'id ibn Jubayr when he saw a man putting his head between the curtain (sitr) and the House (Ka'bah), so he forbade him and said: I heard Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that the muhrim put his head between the curtain and the wall, or between the curtain and the House."
"1463","1199. Ishaq said: Waki' informed us, from Wahb ibn Uqbah, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, that Maymunah shaved her head, meaning due to an ailment in her head."
"1464","Chapter of entering Makkah and its virtue (28). Musaddad said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Yazid Abu Yunus, he is al-Qawi, narrated, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left for Madinah, he walked, then turned toward the House (Ka'bah) and said: By Allah, I do not know of any house that Allah the Exalted placed on earth more beloved to me than you, nor any town more beloved to me than you; and I did not leave you out of dislike, but those who disbelieved drove me out. Then he called out: O Banu Abd Manaf, it is not lawful for any servant to prevent any servant from circumambulating this House at whatever hour he wishes, by night or by day."
"1465","1201. Ibn Abi Umar said: Hisham, he is Ibn Sulayman, narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he narrated from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Hands are raised in seven places: at the beginning of prayer, when you see the House (Ka'bah), on al-Safa and al-Marwah, on the evening of Arafah, at Muzdalifah (Jam'), at the two Jamarat, and over the deceased."
"1466","Chapter of selling the houses of Makkah (29). 1202. Musaddad said: Fudayl narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O people of Makkah, do not put doors on your houses so that the desert-dweller may stay wherever he wishes."
"1467","Chapter of tawaf riding (30). 1203. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Rawh narrated, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, my brother Abdullah ibn Ubaydah narrated, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tawaf on his mount on the day of the conquest of Makkah, touching the corners with a curved staff (mihjan) he had with him."
"1468","Chapter of the boundary of the Haram (31). 1204. Ibn Abi Umar said: Hisham ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated, from Muhammad ibn al-Aswad, that he informed him, that Ibrahim the Prophet (peace be upon him) was the first to set up the markers (ansab) of the Haram; Jibril pointed out their places to him."
"1469","1205. He said: And he also informed me: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the conquest of Makkah ordered Tamim ibn Asid, the grandfather of Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Muttalib ibn Tamim, and he demarcated it."
"1470","1206. And he also said: Nasr ibn Thabit narrated to us, from Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father who said: Quraysh found in the Jahiliyyah a stone at the Maqam of Ibrahim containing writing, and they would show it to whoever came to them of the People of the Book, but they did not know what was in it, until a rabbi from Yemen came to them and read it to them. In it was: I am Allah, Lord of Bakkah; I made it when I made the sun and the moon, and I blessed its people in meat and milk. And on the other side: I am Allah, Lord of Bakkah; I created kinship (al-rahim) and derived for it (a name) from My Name; whoever joins it I shall join him, and whoever cuts it I shall cut him off. And on the other side: I am Allah, Lord of Bakkah; I created good and evil, so glad tidings to whoever good comes at his hands, and woe to whoever evil comes at his hands."
"1471","1207. Abu Nu'aym said in al-Ma'rifah: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Ja'far, he is Abu al-Shaykh, narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Rustah narrated, Amr ibn Malik al-Rasibi narrated, al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated, Ibn Khuthaym narrated, Abu al-Tufayl narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Tamim ibn Asid al-Khuza'i to renew the markers (ansab) of the Haram; and Ibrahim (peace be upon him) had placed them, Jibril (peace and blessings be upon him) showing them to him."
"1472","1208. Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn al-Dahhak narrated to us, my father narrated, Imran al-Qattan narrated, from Mutarrif, from Talhah, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever deems anything of the boundaries of Makkah lawful (to violate), upon him is the curse of Allah, the hadith."
"1473","Chapter of the dislike of renting the houses of Makkah during the Hajj season (32). 1209. Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ziyad narrated, I heard Ibn Jurayj narrating, from Ata, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both): that one who consumes the rent of the houses of Makkah only consumes fire in his belly."
"1474","1210. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hushaym narrated to us, al-Hajjaj informed us, from Ata, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The wages (rents) of the houses of Makkah were forbidden, and the sale of its dwellings."
"1475","Chapter of speech during tawaf (33). 1211. Ishaq said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah informed us, Sufyan narrated, from a man of the people of Makkah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: While I was circumambulating with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he stopped and smiled. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw you stop and smile? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I met Isa (Jesus) circumambulating with two angels with him; he greeted me, and I greeted him."
"1476","1212/1. Abu Ya'la said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, Sharik narrated, from Asim ibn Ubaydillah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, from his father who said: I saw Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) circumambulating the House while chanting (hida') and wearing leather socks (khuffs). Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: I do not know which is more astonishing, your chanting around the House or your tawaf in your sandals. He said: I did this in the time of one better than you, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not find fault with it in me."
"1477","1212/2. Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Sharik narrated, and he mentioned it."
"1478","Chapter of tawaf in leather socks and sandals (34). 1213/1. Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi said: Umar ibn Qays Sandal narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydillah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, from his father who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in tawaf when his sandal broke. I said: O Messenger of Allah, hand it to me so I may fix it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is preferring (itharah), and I do not like preferring (being singled out)."
"1479","1213/2. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami and Ishaq, he is Ibn Abi Isra'il, narrated to us; they said: Umar ibn Ali ibn Muqaddam narrated, Umar, freedman of Banu Manzur ibn Sayyar, narrated, Asim ibn Ubaydillah narrated, and he mentioned it with the wording: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was circumambulating the House when his sandal strap broke; a man took out a strap from his own sandal and went to tie it on the sandal of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he pulled it away and said: This is preferring, and I do not like preferring."
"1480","Chapter of what to say in tawaf (35). 1214. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated to me, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi', from Habib ibn Suhban who said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with them both) circumambulating the House, saying between the Door and the Corner, or between the Maqam and the Door: {Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and shield us from the punishment of the Fire}."
"1481","Chapter of tawaf for one riding (36). 1215. Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Ata informed me: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered his wife to perform tawaf riding in her curtained enclosure (khiDr), behind those praying, inside the mosque. I said: By night or by day? He said: I do not know. I said: In which of the seven (circuits)? He said: I do not know. I (Ibn Hajar) say: This is mursal; its men are men of the Sahih. Its origin is connected (mawsul) with the two Shaykhs in the two Sahihs from the narration of Urwah, from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, from Umm Salamah; and in some of its paths that it was the dawn prayer and that it was the farewell tawaf."
"1482","Chapter of the virtue of tawaf (37). 1216. Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn Hammad narrated to us, al-Husayn, meaning al-Ju'fi, narrated, from Ibn al-Sammak, from A'idh, from Ata, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted boasts of those performing tawaf. This A'idh is Ibn Nusayr, with nun and unpointed letter, diminutive, weak; and Ibn al-Sammak, Muhammad ibn Subayh, is also weak."
"1483","1217. Abu Ya'la said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hammad ibn al-Ja'di narrated, from Qatadah, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, that a freedwoman of Abdullah ibn Amr narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet of Allah who said: Whoever circumambulates the House seven times and prays two rak'ahs behind the Maqam, it is like freeing a slave."
"1484","Chapter of joining tawafs (38). 1218. Musaddad said: Isma'il narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib ibn Barakah, from his mother, that A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) would perform three weeks (asabi', i.e. three sets of seven circuits) joining them together, then pray two rak'ahs for each week."
"1485","1219. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Jami' al-Attar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uthman narrated, Abd al-Salam ibn Abi al-Janub narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tawaf before dawn, then prayed six rak'ahs, turning right and left in every two rak'ahs. He said: So we thought it was two rak'ahs for each week, and he did not give salam. Its isnad is weak."
"1486","Chapter of crowding to kiss the Black Stone and its virtue (39). 1220. Abu Ya'la said: Shayban, he is Ibn Farrukh, narrated to us, Jarir, he is Ibn Hazim, narrated, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both): that he would crowd at the Corner, and when they saw him they made room for him. Nafi' said: I once fell into the crush of people; a man put his elbow behind me, and the man in front of him fell, and I fell behind; I did not think I would turn over until they killed me, while he insisted on going forward."
"1487","1221. Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): that when he passed by the Black Stone and saw crowding over it, he would face it and say takbir, and say: O Allah, out of faith in You and the Sunnah of Your Prophet."
"1488","1222. Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), raised (to the Prophet): Were it not for what the filth of the Jahiliyyah touched it with, no one afflicted with an ailment would touch it but he would be healed; and there is nothing on earth from Paradise except it."
"1489","1223. Muhammad ibn Abi Umar said: Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, I heard Ibn Jurayj say: I heard Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Ja'far say: I heard Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: This Corner is the right hand of Allah on earth, with which He shakes hands with His servants as a man shakes hands with his brother. This is mawquf sahih."
"1490","1224. Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn Uqbah narrated, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: An angel descended with the Black Stone."
"1491","1225. Al-Harith said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: What did you do regarding touching the Stone? He said: I said: I touched and I left off. He said: You did right. Its narrators are trustworthy; if Urwah heard it from Abd al-Rahman (may Allah be pleased with him), then it is sahih. Al-Shafi'i carried it to mean that he did not touch at times of crowding, and touched at other times."
"1492","1226. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Umar ibn Sa'id narrated to me, Manbudh narrated to me, from his mother who said: I was with A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) when a freedwoman of hers came and said: I touched the Stone three times in seven (circuits) I circumambulated. She said: May Allah not reward you, two or three times! Why did you not say takbir and make a knot (gesture) and pass by? You wanted to jostle men."
"1493","Chapter of what is recited in the two rak'ahs of tawaf (40). 1227. Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Ya'qub ibn Zayd who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in the two rak'ahs of tawaf: {Say: O disbelievers} and {Say: He is Allah, One}. This is mursal, and Musa is weak."
"1494","Chapter of prostrating on the Black Stone (41). 1228. Ishaq said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa informed us, Hanzalah narrated, from Tawus who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) would kiss the Stone, then prostrate on it, then kiss it, then prostrate on it three times, the hadith. The rest of it they transmitted."
"1495","1229. Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi said: Ja'far ibn Abdillah ibn Uthman al-Qurashi, of the people of Makkah, narrated to us; he said: I saw Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Ja'far kiss the Stone and prostrate on it, and he said: I saw my maternal uncle Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) kiss it and prostrate on it, and he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) kiss the Stone and prostrate on it."
"1496","1230. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated, from Sharik ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Namir, from Isa ibn Talhah, from a man who saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stand at the Stone and say: I know that you are a stone that neither harms nor benefits, then he kissed it. Then Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) performed Hajj and stood at the Stone, then said: I know that you are a stone that neither harms nor benefits, and were it not that I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kissing you I would not have kissed you."
"1497","Chapter of the woman's tawaf (42). 1231. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, al-Hasan ibn Muslim narrated, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah who said: A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) would circumambulate the House veiled (munaqqabah). He said: And Ata disliked it, until I narrated this hadith to him, after which he would give fatwa by it."
"1498","Chapter of standing at Arafah and departing (43). 1232. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Hajj is Arafah, and umrah is tawaf."
"1499","1233. Musaddad also said: Khalid, he is al-Tahhan, narrated to us, Humayd, he is al-Tawil, narrated, from Bakr, he is Ibn Abdillah al-Muzani, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Whoever reaches Arafah has reached Hajj, and whoever misses Arafah has missed Hajj. (Sahih mawquf)."
"1500","1234. Ishaq: Jarir informed us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Rabi'ah al-Qurashi, from his father who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing in the Jahiliyyah at Arafat with the polytheists, and I saw him standing in Islam at that same standing-place, so I knew that Allah, Blessed and Exalted, kept him there for that. This is a hadith with a strange isnad."
"1501","1235. Abu Bakr: Waki' narrated to us, from Nafi' ibn Umar, from Sa'id ibn Hassan, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would stay in the valley of Namirah."
"1502","1236/1. Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril came to Ibrahim (peace and blessings be upon them both) and set out with him to Mina, and prayed all the prayers with him there, then prayed Fajr with him, then went with him in the morning to Arafah, alighted with him where people alight, then prayed the two prayers together with him, then brought him to the standing-place, until when it was as early as the earliest anyone prays Maghrib he departed (afada); he brought him to Muzdalifah (Jam') and prayed the two Isha's together with him, then spent the night until when it was as early as the earliest anyone prays Fajr he prayed Fajr with him, then stood with him, until when it was as late as the latest anyone prays he departed with him to Mina, stoned the Jamrah, then sacrificed and shaved, then went with him to the House (for tawaf). Then Allah the Exalted thereafter revealed to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Then follow the religion of Ibrahim, inclining to truth}."
"1503","1236/2. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah who said: A man of Quraysh said to Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both): I am weak with family and mounts, and our mounts are these ambling donkeys; may I depart from Muzdalifah (Jam') by night? He said: As for Ibrahim, he spent the night at Mina until when morning came and the sun's edge rose he set out to Arafah until he alighted at a place there, then set out in the afternoon, then stood at his standing-place there until the sun set, then departed, until when he came to Muzdalifah he alighted at his place there, until when it was the hastened dawn prayer he stood, until when it was bright dawn he departed. That is the religion of Ibrahim (peace be upon him), and your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to follow him."
"1504","1236/3. Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us, Isma'il narrated with this."
"1505","1236/4. Ibn Abi Umar said: Ibrahim ibn Sulayman ibn Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Nawfali narrated to us, from a man, from al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Jibril (peace and blessings be upon him) departed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he came to Muzdalifah, alighted there and spent the night, then prayed dawn as early as the earliest any Muslim prays, then stood with him as late as the latest any Muslim stands, then moved to Mina, stoned and sacrificed, then Allah, Blessed and Exalted, revealed to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Then We revealed to you: Follow the religion of Ibrahim, inclining to truth}, the verse."
"1506","1237. Al-Harith: Yazid, he is Ibn Harun, narrated to us, al-Awwam ibn Hawshab narrated, al-Saffah ibn Matar narrated to me, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdillah ibn Khalid ibn Asid (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Arafah is the day people know (gather)."
"1507","1238. Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Salih ibn Khawwat narrated, from Yazid ibn Ruman, from Habib ibn Umayr, from Habib ibn Khumashah al-Khatmi (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say at Arafah: All of Arafah is a standing-place except the valley of Uranah; and all of Muzdalifah is a standing-place except the valley of Muhassir."
"1508","Chapter of supplication on the day of Arafah and its virtue (44). 1239. Ishaq: Waki' informed us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, from Abdullah ibn Ubaydah, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most frequent of my supplication and the supplication of the Prophets before me at Arafah is: There is no god but Allah, alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things. O Allah, place light in my hearing, light in my sight, and light in my heart. O Allah, expand my chest for me and ease my affair for me. And I seek refuge in You from the whisperings of breasts and the scattering of affairs. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the evil of what enters in the night, and from the evil of what enters in the day, and from the evil of what the winds blow with, and from the evil of the calamities of time."
"1509","1240. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Jabalah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Marwan narrated, from Hisham, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There are no days better with Allah the Exalted than the days of the ten of Dhu al-Hijjah. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, are they better, or their number in jihad in the path of Allah the Exalted? He said: They are better than their number in jihad in the path of Allah, except a dusty one (a martyr) rolled in dust. And there is no day better with Allah the Exalted than the day of Arafah; Allah the Exalted descends to the lowest heaven and says: Look at My servants, disheveled and dusty, pilgrims, come from every deep mountain pass; they have not seen My mercy nor My punishment. And no day is seen with more freed from the Fire than the day of Arafah."
"1510","1241. Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Ishaq narrated, Azrah ibn Qays narrated, Umm al-Fayd narrated to me that she heard Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) say, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever says on the night of Arafah these ten words a thousand times, he will not ask Allah the Exalted for anything but He will give it to him, except severing kinship or sin: Glory be to Him Whose Throne is in the heaven; glory be to Him Whose foothold is on the earth; glory be to Him Whose path is in the sea; glory be to Him Whose authority is in the Fire; glory be to Him Whose mercy is in Paradise; glory be to Him Whose decree is in the graves; glory be to Him Whose blessing is in the air; glory be to Him Who raised the heaven; glory be to Him Who laid out the earth; glory be to Him from Whom there is no refuge except to Him."
"1511","1242. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Muslim narrated, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was seen on the evening of Arafah raising his hands, so that what was under his armpits could be seen."
"1512","1243/1. Surayj ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Bishr ibn Harb, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood at Arafah and began supplicating like this, with the backs of his hands toward his chest."
"1513","1243/2. Abu Nasr narrated to us, Hammad narrated, with the wording: He stood at Arafat, and said like this... and he raised his hands toward his beard (thunduwah)."
"1514","1244. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, Ayyash al-Kalbi narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Babah who said: I heard Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, boasts of the people of Arafah to the angels."
"1515","1245. Bishr, he is Ibn al-Mufaddal, narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Salim narrated, from Sa'id, raised (to the Prophet): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) on the morning of Muzdalifah (Jam') to call out among the people: Be silent, or: Keep quiet. He did so, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted has bestowed favor upon you in your gathering, so He has gifted your wrongdoers to your righteous, and has granted your righteous what they asked. Move on in the name of Allah."
"1516","1246. Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, my father narrated, Talib ibn Salm narrated, our family narrated to me that they heard my grandfather say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on that day: Verily Allah the Exalted looked at this gathering and accepted from their righteous, and interceded their righteous for their wrongdoers, and pardoned them all."
"1517","1247. Abd (ibn Humayd) said: al-Qasim ibn al-Walid narrated to us, al-Sabbah ibn Musa narrated, from Abu Dawud al-Subay'i, from Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No one remains on the day of Arafah with a mustard-seed's weight of faith in his heart but Allah the Exalted forgives him. A man said: Is it for the people of al-Mu'arraf (those gathered), O Messenger of Allah, or for people in general? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather, for people in general."
"1518","1248/1. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Shuja' ibn Abi Nasr al-Balkhi narrated to us, Salih al-Murri narrated, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted boasts of the people of Arafat to the angels, saying: O My angels, look at My servants; they have come hastening to Me from every deep mountain pass, disheveled and dusty. Bear witness that I have answered their supplication, gifted their wrongdoers to their righteous, and given their righteous what they asked, except for claims (tabi'at) between them. Then when they depart and come to Muzdalifah (Jam') they beseech Allah the Exalted again in asking, so Allah the Exalted says: O My angels, My servants have returned to Me in asking; bear witness that I have answered their supplication, gifted their wrongdoers to their righteous, given their righteous what they asked of Me, and taken upon Myself the claims between them."
"1519","1248/2. Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Salih, he is al-Murri, narrated the like of it; and in it: And I gave their righteous all that they asked Me, except the claims between them. And in it: My angels, My servants stood then returned to desire and asking, so I call you to witness that I have answered their supplication, to the end of it."
"1520","Chapter of departing from Muzdalifah (45). 1249. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, Nafi' informed me that Ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with them both) departed early (isfar); so Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: Sunrise? Are they waiting for the practice of the people of the Jahiliyyah? Then Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) departed, and the people departed with him, and Ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with them both) departed. This is mawquf sahih, and it has the ruling of marfu'."
"1521","1250. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i narrated to us, Abu Amir, he is al-Aqadi, narrated, Zam'ah, he is Ibn Salih, narrated, from Salamah, he is Ibn Wahram, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The people of the Jahiliyyah would stand at Arafat until when the sun was over the mountain tops like turbans on men's heads they would depart; then they would stand at Muzdalifah until when the sun was over the mountain tops they would move. When Islam came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the departure from Arafat until the sun set, and hastened the departure from Muzdalifah (Jam'), departing from it when everything had brightened (isfar) at the last time, and praying there in darkness (ghalas). Ahmad transmitted it abridged, from Abu Dawud, from Zam'ah."
"1522","1251. Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas who said: I departed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on both departures, and he would depart with tranquility (sakinah) upon him."
"1523","Chapter of staying at Mina (46). 1252. Musaddad: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When we arrive, God willing, we shall stay at al-Khayf; and al-Khayf is the mosque of Mina. This is mursal."
"1524","Chapter of the virtue of shaving (47). 1253. Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah, from al-Harith ibn Abdillah ibn Ka'b, from Umm Umarah Nusaybah bint Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I was looking at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was slaughtering his sacrificial camels standing, and I heard him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, after he had shaved his head, then entered a red tent of his, so I saw him bring his head out of his tent saying: May Allah have mercy on those who shave, three times; then he said: And those who shorten."
"1525","Chapter of spending the night at Mina (48). 1254. Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, Ayyub ibn Sinan narrated, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas who said: No one was permitted to spend the night away from Mina except al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, because of his watering (siqayah)."
"1526","Chapter of stoning the Jamarat (49). 1255. Abu Bakr said: Mahbub al-Fazari narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Amir al-Aslami, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When we reached the valley of Muhassir, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take the pebbles of the Jamarat from the valley of Muhassir."
"1527","1256. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Harun ibn Abi A'ishah narrated to me, from Adi ibn Adi al-Kindi, from Salman ibn Rabi'ah who said: We looked at Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) on the day of the first departure (al-nafr al-awwal); he came out to us with his beard dripping water, with pebbles in his hand and pebbles in his garment-fold (huzzah), walking, saying takbir on his way until he came to the first Jamrah, stoned it until the pebbles ran out, fearing to be hit by the pebbles of those stoning, then supplicated a while, then went to the middle Jamrah, then the last one."
"1528","1257. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah, from Ibn Abi Nu'm, from Abu Sa'id who said: The pebbles of the Jamarat: what is accepted of them is raised, and what is rejected is left; and were it not for that, they would be taller than a span."
"1529","1258. Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf, freedman of the family of Uthman, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman, he heard him report that he heard Abu Habbah give fatwa to people: that there is no harm in what a man has stoned with, of pebbles or other, in the Jamarat. Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman said: That was mentioned to Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and he said: He spoke the truth. And Abu Habbah (may Allah be pleased with him) was a Badri."
"1530","1259. Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Humayd al-A'raj, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from a man of his people called Mu'adh, or Ibn Mu'adh, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught people their rites; he said: Allah the Exalted opened our hearing, for we would hear while in our dwellings. Among what he taught us was: When you stone the Jamrah, stone it with the like of the pebbles of khadhf (small flung pebbles). Abu Dawud transmitted part of it from the narration of Abd al-Rahman ibn Mu'adh al-Taymi, from a man of the Companions (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1531","1260. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Abdillah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted shepherds to stone the Jamarat by night."
"1532","Chapter of the sacrificial offering (hady) (50). 1261. Abu Ya'la said: Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir narrated, from Isma'il, from al-Hasan, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) driving his sacrificial camel barefoot, and he said: Ride it! So he rode it. I (Ibn Hajar) say: It is in the Sahih from the hadith of Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) without his words: barefoot."
"1533","1262. Abu Dawud said: Abu Isra'il narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from al-Mughirah ibn Hadhaf, from Ali, or Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made Muslims partners in their sacrificial animals: a cow for seven."
"1534","1263. Musaddad: Khalid narrated to us, from Khalid, from Amr ibn Sa'id, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would send the sacrificial offering, ordering the one he sent it with that if any of it became incapacitated, he should slaughter it, dye its sandal (strap) in its blood, then strike its side with it; and those after you may eat it, but you yourself shall eat nothing of it, nor anyone of your company. He said: And Muhammad ibn Sirin would do that."
"1535","1264. Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both): regarding a man who sends the sacrificial offering while he is resident? He said: He appoints a day with him (the carrier); when it (the day) arrives, he (the sender) refrains from what the one in ihram refrains from. [Sahih mawquf]."
"1536","1265. Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit narrated to me, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) would slaughter camels hobbled on the left foreleg, standing on their remaining legs."
"1537","1266. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Bahr narrated to us, Sulaym ibn Muslim narrated, Ibn Abi Layla narrated, from Ata, from Abu al-Khalil, from Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding voluntary sacrificial camels: When it becomes incapacitated before entering the Haram, slaughter it, then dip your hand in its blood, then strike its side; and do not eat of it, for if you eat of it you must compensate for it."
"1538","1267. Amr ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated, Ibn Jurayj narrated, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, a sacrificial camel became due upon me, but the camels have been slaughtered; what do you think? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Slaughter seven sheep in its place."
"1539","1268. Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musa al-Fitri narrated, from Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali, from his father, from his grandfather who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drove a hundred sacrificial camels in his Hajj."
"1540","Chapter of the talbiyah, when it ends, and whether it is said in sanctified places (51). 1269. Ishaq said: Ya'la informed us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated, from Muhammad ibn Shihab, from Yahya ibn Abbad, from Abbad who said: I was told that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), when he entered Bayt al-Maqdis, said: Labbayk, O Allah, Labbayk!"
"1541","1270. Ishaq said: Ahmad ibn Ayyub informed us, from Abu Hamzah al-Sukkari, from Jabir, he is al-Ju'fi, from Mujahid, from A'ishah who said: I heard nothing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except these words: Labbayk, O Allah, Labbayk; Labbayk, no partner have You; indeed praise and blessing belong to You. And I never heard him mention Hajj or umrah. Mujahid said: And Umar ibn al-Khattab said in it: And dominion, no partner have You. I (Ibn Hajar) say: It is in the Sahih from the hadith of A'ishah with the addition, without her words: mention Hajj or umrah."
"1542","1271. Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, Hisham ibn Hassan narrated, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Yahya ibn Sirin: that he performed Hajj with Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), and he would say in his talbiyah: Labbayk, a true Hajj, in worship and servitude."
"1543","1272. Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from a man, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): that he departed from Arafah, and his talbiyah was: Labbayk, O Allah, Labbayk!"
"1544","1273/1. Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Abu Hanifah, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The best of Hajj is al-ajj and al-thajj; al-ajj is raising the voice (in talbiyah), and al-thajj is slaughtering."
"1545","1273/2. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated with this, except that he said: As for al-ajj, it is the talbiyah; and as for al-thajj, it is slaughtering camels."
"1546","1274. Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Numayr narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Isma'il, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say: Labbayk, O Allah, Labbayk; Labbayk, no partner have You, Labbayk; indeed praise and blessing and dominion belong to You, no partner have You!"
"1547","Chapter of the sermon on the day of sacrifice and the day after it (52). 1275/1. Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, my father narrated, Rabi'ah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Husayn al-Ghanawi narrated, my grandmother al-Sarra bint Nabhan ibn Amr, who was a house-mistress in the Jahiliyyah, narrated to me; she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Hajj say: Do you know what day this is? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: This is the middle of the days of Tashriq. He said: Do you know what town this is? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: This is al-Mash'ar al-Haram. He said: I do not know, perhaps I shall not meet you after this year of mine. Verily your blood, your wealth, and your honor are sacred among you, some of you over others, like the sanctity of this day of yours in this town of yours, until you meet Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and He asks you about your deeds. Let the nearest of you convey to the farthest? He said: Then he followed it with: O Allah, have I conveyed? O Allah, have I conveyed? Then he passed away when he reached Madinah."
"1548","1275/2. Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ar'arah and Ya'qub narrated to us, separating them; they said: Abu Asim narrated with it. Abu Dawud transmitted it abridged from the hadith of Abu Asim with this isnad."
"1549","Chapter of the recompense for game and its prohibition for the muhrim (53). 1276. Musaddad: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Khuthaym, Yusuf ibn Mahak informed me that he heard Abdullah ibn Abi Ammar say: I set out with Mu'adh ibn Jabal and Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with them both) in ihram for umrah from Bayt al-Maqdis; our leader was Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) and our affair was to him, and he would lead us in prayer. When we were partway, Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) went aside to relieve himself, and a man opposed him with a wild donkey he had hamstrung; Ka'b took it and gifted it to the company. Mu'adh did not return but the pots of the people were boiling with some of it. He asked, was informed, and said: None shall obey me but he must overturn his pot! So Ka'b and the people overturned their pots. When we were partway, with Ka'b praying by a fire, a foot (swarm) of locusts passed by him; he took two locusts, killed them, forgetting his ihram, then remembered his ihram and threw them away. When we reached Madinah, the people entered upon Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and I entered with them. Ka'b said: What do you think, O Commander of the Faithful, and told him the story of the two locusts? He said: What harm is there in that, O Ka'b? He said: Yes. He said: Himyar loves locusts; and what did you impose upon yourself? He said: Two dirhams. He said: Two dirhams are better than a hundred locusts; impose what you imposed upon yourself."
"1550","1277. Yahya narrated to us, Salim ibn Hilal narrated, Abu al-Siddiq narrated to me, from Abu Sa'id, that he performed Hajj with Ka'b; locusts came, and Ka'b began striking with a whip. I said: O Abu Ishaq! Are you not in ihram? He said: Yes indeed; it is of sea game; its first only emerged from the nostril of a whale."
"1551","1278. Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith, from Isa ibn Talhah, from his father who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Sifah al-Rawha' when we came upon a hamstrung donkey; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This donkey, its owner is about to come. It was not long before its owner came and said: Take it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to distribute it among the companies. Then we set out until, when we were at al-Uthayah at al-Arj, there was a gazelle crouching with a deeply embedded arrow in it; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to stand over it and keep people away from it. The owner of the donkey was a man of Bahz. I (Ibn Hajar) say: The outward appearance of this isnad is sound, but it is defective (ma'lul). Ali ibn al-Madini explained that in the Book of Defects (al-Ilal), that he said to Ibn Uyaynah: People contradict you; they do not say: from Isa ibn Talhah, from his father! He said: I have told you the hadith, and I thought it was from his father. Ali said: The correct is: from Isa ibn Talhah, from al-Bahzi."
"1552","1279. Musaddad said: Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, Hisham narrated, from his father who said: al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) would travel with dried wild game (safif al-wahsh) and eat it while in ihram. Sahih mawquf."
"1553","1280. Al-Harith: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn al-Fudayl al-Khatmi narrated, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Dhu'ayb al-Asadi who said: I accompanied al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him) from Madinah to Makkah while he was in ihram; and he would eat land game meat. I spoke to him about that, and he said: A non-muhrim hunted it; and I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he saw no harm in it."
"1554","1281/1. Musaddad: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) ruled: for the jerboa, a young goat (jafrah); for the hyena, a ram; for the gazelle, a sheep; and for the rabbit, a young she-goat (anaq)."
"1555","1281/2. Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Ayyub narrated, Abu al-Zubayr narrated with it. 1281/3. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Sufyan narrated to us with it, confining himself to the jerboa."
"1556","1282. Abu Ya'la: Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, Malik ibn Su'ayr narrated, from al-Ajlah, from Abu al-Zubayr, and he mentioned it, but added: and I think he only raised it (to the Prophet)."
"1557","1283. Musaddad: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Abd al-Hamid ibn Jubayr narrated to me; he said: Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said regarding ostrich eggs struck by the muhrim: Put the stallion camel upon your she-camels; when their pregnancy becomes clear to you, name the number of eggs you struck. I said: This is sacrificial offering (hady); its guarantee is not upon you; what of it is sound is sound, and what perishes is not upon you, like eggs: some are sound and some spoil. Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) marveled at Ali's (may Allah be pleased with him) ruling, so Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: What did not please Mu'awiyah is this, except what eggs are sold for in the market and given in charity."
"1558","1284. Abdullah narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Ikrimah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled a ram for the hyena, and made it game."
"1559","1285. Ibn Abi Umar said: Yusuf ibn Khalid narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Ya'la ibn Ata, from Isma'il, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled regarding the hunting dog, when struck, forty dirhams; for the sheepdog, a sheep from sheep; for the farm dog, a faraq of food; and for the house dog, a faraq of dust; it is a duty upon the killer's master to pay it, and a duty upon the householder to accept it. This isnad is very feeble (wahin jiddan)."
"1560","Chapter of umrah (54). 1286/1. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hushaym narrated to us, Abu Bishr informed us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from a woman of the Ansar called Umm Sinan (may Allah be pleased with her), that she intended Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said to her: Perform umrah in Ramadan, for it is for you (equal to) a Hajj. Sa'id said: And we know it only for this woman alone."
"1561","1286/2. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Isma'il narrated to us, Ayyub narrated, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, with its meaning."
"1562","Chapter of performing umrah in the ten of Dhu al-Hijjah (55). 1287. Ishaq said: Sulayman ibn Harb informed me, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah who said: Urwah said to Ibn Abbas: Woe to you! Are you misguided? You command umrah in the ten, and there is no umrah in them!? He said: O Urayya, ask your mother! He said: Abu Bakr and Umar did not say that; and they were more knowledgeable of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and more following of him than you. He said: From here you are stoned (refuted): we bring you the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and you bring Abu Bakr and Umar! Its isnad is sahih; and part of it connected with umrah is in Sahih Muslim, to which Ibn Abbas's statement in the two Sahihs points: the Sunnah of Abu al-Qasim, when Abu Jamrah said to him that he saw in a dream one saying to him: an accepted umrah, or an accepted mut'ah."
"1563","Chapter of performing umrah from Bayt al-Maqdis (56). 1288. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Khuthaym, Yusuf ibn Mahak informed me that he heard Abdullah ibn Abi Ammar say: I set out with Mu'adh ibn Jabal and Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with them both) in ihram for umrah from Bayt al-Maqdis, the hadith."
"1564","Chapter of the farewell tawaf (57). 1289. Musaddad: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Abi Layla narrated, from Ata (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs Hajj of this House, let his last covenant be tawaf of the House; and he permitted women (concession)."
"1565","1290. Ishaq said: Waki' informed me, Mis'ar narrated, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Tawus who said: I never saw Ibn Abbas remain silent when anyone contradicted him, until... then Jabir ibn Abdillah contradicted him regarding the menstruating woman after she has circumambulated on the day of sacrifice; Ibn Abbas said: She departs. They sent to a woman to whom that had happened in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she agreed with Ibn Abbas. I (Ibn Hajar) say: Its origin is in the Sahih without mention of Jabir; and she was named Umm Sulaym."
"1566","Chapter of the lawfulness of meeting the pilgrim and congratulating him on his safety (58). 1291. Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from al-Muhajir who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The pilgrim is forgiven, and those for whom the pilgrim seeks forgiveness, for the rest of Dhu al-Hijjah, Muharram, Safar, and ten (days) of Rabi' al-Awwal."
"1567","1292. Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Kathir informed me, from Wahb ibn Kaysan who said: I saw Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) pray with people in Madinah in the evening, on the day of the last departure (al-nafr al-akhir); then he said: Verily Muhammad Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has outstripped with good deeds, and Dhakwan, freedman of Marwan, has outstripped the pilgrims, and informed of the people's safety. Sufyan said: And regarding that Dhakwan says: I burdened it... This isnad to Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) is upon the condition of the Sahih, and it is mawquf."
"1568","Chapter of the virtues of the Ka'bah and the Sacred Mosque (59). 1593. Ishaq: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated, from al-Hasan who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) intended to take the treasure of the Ka'bah and spend it in the path of Allah the Exalted, but Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: Your two companions preceded you and did not do it; had it been good they would have done it. So he left it. This is munqati'."
"1569","1294. Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna said in the additions of Musaddad: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated, from al-Mughirah ibn Ziyad, from Ata who said: The Throne is over the Haram."
"1570","1295. Al-Harith: Ahmad ibn Yazid Abu Abdillah, of the people of Kirman, narrated to us, Sa'id narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted sends down every day a hundred mercies: sixty of them for those circumambulating, twenty of them for the people of Makkah, and twenty of them for the rest of people."
"1571","1296. Al-Fakihi said: al-Hasan ibn Ali narrated to us, Amr ibn Asim narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Yusuf ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I know the most beloved spot on earth to Allah the Exalted, and it is the House (Ka'bah) and what is around it."
"1572","1297. Maymun ibn al-Hakam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ju'shum narrated, from Ibn Jurayj who said: I heard Ibn Abi Husayn say: Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, chose speech, so He chose the Qur'an; and chose lands, so He chose the Haram; and chose the Haram, so He chose the Mosque; and chose the Mosque, so He chose the place of the House (Ka'bah)."
"1573","Chapter of the covering (kiswah) of the Ka'bah (60). 1298. Al-Harith: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Rashid narrated, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade reviling As'ad al-Himyari, and said: He is the first who clothed the House (Ka'bah). Al-Waqidi alone transmitted it, and he is weak."
"1574","1299. Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabrah narrated, from Khalid ibn Rabah, from al-Muttalib ibn Abdillah ibn Hantab, from Mina who said: I heard al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clothed the House (Ka'bah) in his Hajj with striped Yemeni garments (hibarat)."
"1575","Chapter of prayer inside the House (61). 1300. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, my father narrated to me; he said: Ali ibn al-Husayn (may Allah be pleased with them both) was asked about prayer inside the Ka'bah, and he said: I prayed with my father al-Husayn ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) inside the Ka'bah. This is a sahih mawquf isnad."
"1576","1301. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah who said: Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) came to Makkah and entered the Ka'bah; he sent to Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and said: Where did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray? He said: Between the two pillars. Then Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with them both) came and shook the door violently; it was opened for him, and he said: O Mu'awiyah, by Allah, you knew that I knew the like of what Ibn Umar knew, but you envied me that you send to me."
"1577","Chapter explaining that entering the House is not obligatory (62). 1302. Ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from Jabir, from Muhammad, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me one day and said: Today I did something I wish I had not done: I entered the House (Ka'bah), and I fear that a man may come from a distant horizon unable to enter it, and return with something of it in himself."
"1578","Chapter of sa'y (63). 1303. Al-Harith: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Musa ibn Damrah ibn Sa'id narrated, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Utbah, from Zayd ibn Khalid (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reach al-Safa, begin with it by day, stand at it, and keep walking until he reached the valley's belly, then run; and people ran with him until he passed the valley, then walked."
"1579","1304. Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated, from Budayl ibn Maysarah, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from a freedwoman (umm walad) of Shaybah who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performing sa'y between al-Safa and al-Marwah, saying: The valley (bathaa') is not crossed except running (shaddan)."
"1580","1305. Bishr, he is Ibn al-Sari, narrated to us, Talhah ibn Umar narrated, from Ata, from Ibn Umm Maktum who said: He performed tawaf with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) between al-Safa and al-Marwah; Ibn Umm Maktum descended and ran, then stood until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caught up with him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And how excellent it is."
"1581","1306/1. Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Hisham narrated, from Budayl, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah who said: I was in a small door (khawkhah) of mine and saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performing sa'y between al-Safa and al-Marwah; and I saw him, when he came to the valley's belly, running until his knees showed."
"1582","1306/2. Musaddad: Hammad narrated to us, Budayl ibn Maysarah narrated, from al-Mughirah ibn Hakim, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from a woman of them, that she saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from a small door (khawkhah) of hers while he was running in the valley's course (batn al-sayl), saying... and he mentioned like the first."
"1583","1307. Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from al-Walid ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Mughith, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from a woman of Banu Nawfal who said: She looked out from a small door (khawkhah) of hers and saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah the Exalted has prescribed sa'y upon you, so perform sa'y. And she heard him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say while running: My Lord, forgive and have mercy; indeed You are the Mightiest, the Most Generous!"
"1584","1308. Musaddad: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: O people of Makkah, your tawaf between al-Safa and al-Marwah is only when you return from Mina."
"1585","Chapter of the watering (siqayah) of al-Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) (64). 1309/1. Ishaq: Qabisah ibn Uqbah informed us, Sufyan narrated, from Musa ibn Abi A'ishah, from Abdullah ibn Abi Razin, from his father, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I said to al-Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him): Ask for us the doorkeeping (hijabah) from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: He said: I give you what is better than it: the watering (siqayah); it profits you and you are not burdened by it. I said to Qabisah: So did he ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: He did not add to this, and it can only be that he asked him. This is a hasan isnad. Ahmad ibn Mani' and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated it from Qabisah likewise. And al-Bazzar narrated it from Muhammad ibn Ma'mar, from Qabisah."
"1586","1309/2. Abu Ya'la said: Ubaydullah, he is al-Qawariri, narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr, he is Abu Ahmad, narrated, Sufyan narrated with it similarly, but he did not say: from his father."
"1587","1310. Ishaq: Jarir informed us, from Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us at the watering-place, and went to drink from the basin from which people drink. We said to him: Shall we not bring out for you, for people have plunged their hands into this? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, rather give me to drink from this from which people have drunk. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank from that from which people drink. In it is a break (inqita'); and with them it is from the hadith of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) similarly."
"1588","1311. Abu Ya'la said: Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Yunus, he is Ibn Bukayr, narrated, Ibrahim ibn Isma'il, he is Ibn Mujammi', narrated, from Zayd ibn Ali al-Hashimi, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to the Jamrah he went until he came to the House (Ka'bah), circumambulated it seven times, then came to Zamzam, was brought a bucket of water, performed ablution, then said: Draw (water) for your watering, O Banu Abd al-Muttalib; were it not that people would overpower you over it I would have drawn."
"1589","Chapter of the virtue of Zamzam (65). 1312/1. Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Zamzam is nourishing food and a cure for illness. Sahih; and it is part of the hadith of Abu Dharr's (may Allah be pleased with him) acceptance of Islam; Muslim narrated it at length except this wording: and a cure for illness."
"1590","1312/2. Al-Tayalisi said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, and he mentioned the hadith abridged; and in it: a cure for illness."
"1591","Chapter of the sanctuary (haram) of Madinah and its virtue (66). 1313. Ishaq: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad informed us, Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi narrated, from his father who said: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) found a disobedient woman (asiyah) cutting the sanctuary (hima), so he took her axe and cloak. She complained of him to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Give her back her axe and cloak! He said: By Allah, I shall not give back; it is spoils that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted me! Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) made from that axe a hoe and kept working with it until he died. I (Ibn Hajar) say: Muslim and others narrated it from other than this path with other than this wording and context. In this is the addition of complaining to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and Umar's (may Allah be pleased with him) affirming him in it. And Muhammad ibn Ibrahim did not hear from Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him); al-Tirmidhi narrated for him a hadith from his narration from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father."
"1592","1314. Ishaq: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad informed us, Zayd ibn Aslam informed me; he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever you find cutting anything of the sanctuary (hima), beat him and strip him."
"1593","1315. Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah narrated to me, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When we returned from the expedition of Tabuk, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is Taybah; my Lord settled me in it; it expels the filth of its people as the bellows expels the filth of iron. Whoever of you meets anyone of those who stayed behind, let him neither speak to him nor sit with him!"
"1594","1316. Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr, he is Ibn Harb, narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, he is Ibn Zubalah, narrated, from Malik, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Cities were conquered by the sword, and Madinah was conquered by the Qur'an. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan alone transmitted it, and he was very weak; this is only the saying of Malik, but Muhammad ibn al-Hasan made it marfu' and produced an isnad for it. Others than Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated it, adding A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) in the isnad."
"1595","1317. Abu Ya'la said: Mus'ab al-Zubayri narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Abdullah ibn Ikrimah, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), from his father, from Subay'ah al-Aslamiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever is able to die in Madinah, let him die there; for no one dies there but I shall be for him an intercessor or a witness on the Day of Resurrection. This is a well-known hadith from this path, but with Sumaytah al-Laythiyyah instead of Subay'ah al-Aslamiyyah; al-Nasa'i transmitted it."
"1596","1318 - Al-Harith reported: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, from Shu'bah, who said: Amr ibn Murrah said: Abu al-Bakhtari al-Ta'i informed me, he said: Some people were in Kufah, then he mentioned a hadith, in it: so they came to Madinah, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Allah Almighty chose Madinah for His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); it is the land with the least food and the saltiest water, except for these dates; neither the Dajjal nor the plague will enter it, if Allah Almighty wills."
"1597","67 - Chapter on the merit of Uhud. 1319 - Abd al-Muhaymin ibn Abbas ibn Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi informed us, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When he returned from the expedition of Tabuk and looked upon Thaniyyat al-Mabrak, Uhud appeared before him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is a mountain that loves us and we love it. Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah corroborated him, from Abbas ibn Sahl; al-Bukhari cited it as a suspended report through his chain."
"1598","68 - Chapter on visiting the grave of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 1320/1 - Musaddad reported: Yahya narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Umar ibn Muhammad, from his father, who said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) whenever he returned from a journey would pray two rak'ahs in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then come to the grave and say: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah; peace be upon you, O Abu Bakr; peace be upon you, O my father."
"1599","1320/2 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both): that whenever he returned from a journey he would begin with the mosque, pray two rak'ahs in it, then come to the grave, and he mentioned the like of it. It is authentic, as a stopped report."
"1600","1321 - And Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not turn my grave into an idol."
"1601","1322 - And al-Tayalisi said: Sawwar ibn Maymun Abu al-Jarrah al-Abdi narrated to us, a man from the family of Umar narrated to me, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever visits my grave - or he said: whoever visits me - I shall be a witness or an intercessor for him; and whoever dies in one of the two sanctuaries, Allah Almighty will raise him among the secure on the Day of Resurrection."
"1602","1323 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us, Hafs ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs Hajj and then visits me after my death is like one who visited me in my lifetime."
"1603","1324/1 - And Ibn Abi Shaybah said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Ibrahim - from the descendants of Dhu al-Janahayn - narrated to us, Ali ibn Umar narrated to me, from his father, from Ali ibn al-Husayn (may Allah be pleased with them both), that he saw a man coming to a small window near the grave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), entering it and supplicating, so he called him and said: Shall I not narrate to you a hadith I heard from my father, from my grandfather (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Do not take my grave as a place of festivity, nor your houses as graves, and send blessings upon me, for your prayer and salutation reach me wherever you are."
"1604","1324/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this same report."
"1605","69 - Chapter on the merit of Quba. 1325 - Abu Bakr said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, Yusuf ibn Tahman informed me, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs ablution and perfects it, then comes to the mosque of Quba and prays four rak'ahs in it, that equals an Umrah. Musa is weak; al-Nasa'i and Ibn Majah narrated it through a better chain than this, but with a different wording."
"1606","1326 - And al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Agharr, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to Quba riding and walking."
"1607","1327 - And Musaddad said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Kathir, from a man, who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) came to the mosque of Quba and commanded Abu Layla, saying to him: Keep away from the old worn-out mats, and sweep the mosque with a palm branch. He said: Even if this mosque were in a distant horizon or a far city, we ought to come to it."
"1608","70 - Chapter on the merit of the Prophetic Mosque. 1328 - Al-Harith reported: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Salamah ibn Wardan narrated to us, who said: I heard Abu Sa'id ibn al-Mu'alla, who said: I heard Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A prayer in my mosque is better than a thousand prayers in other mosques, except the Sacred Mosque."
"1609","1329 - And al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Sa'd narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Atiq informed me, who said: I heard Ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) say: A prayer in the Sacred Mosque is better than a hundred thousand prayers elsewhere; Sufyan said: So they hold that prayer in the Sacred Mosque is better than a hundred thousand prayers in other mosques, except the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whose merit over it is by a hundred prayers."
"1610","71 - Chapter on the merit of Ta'if. 1330 - Ishaq reported: Abdullah ibn al-Harith al-Makhzumi informed us, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Insan al-Thaqafi, from Abdullah ibn Abd Rabbih ibn al-Hakam ibn Uthman ibn Bishr, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Wajj is sacred; from it the Lord, Blessed and Exalted, ascended to the lowest heaven on the day He completed the creation of the earth. Al-Makhzumi said: Wajj is the valley of Ta'if."
"1611","72 - Chapter on the merit of the mosque of al-Khayf. 1331 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, Ata narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Seventy prophets prayed in the mosque of al-Khayf, and between Hira and Thabir there are seventy prophets."
"1612","1332 - And Abu Ya'la said: al-Ramadi Abu Bakr narrated to us, Abu Hammam al-Dallal narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The mosque of al-Khayf is the grave of seventy prophets."
"1613","73 - Chapter on the merit of al-Aqsa Mosque. 1333 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: al-Hasan - that is Ibn Waqi' - narrated to us, from Damrah, from Abu Anan al-Lakhmi, from Sulayman ibn Kaysan ibn Abi Isa al-Khurasani, who said: Whoever prays the obligatory prayer in the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis in congregation, it counts as twenty-five thousand prayers for him, and whoever prays it alone, it counts as a thousand prayers for him."
"1614","1334 - And Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, Yahya ibn al-Ala narrated to us, Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Abi Sawdah, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Maymunah bint al-Harith (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: O Messenger of Allah, give us a ruling about Bayt al-Maqdis? He said: It is the land of the gathering and the resurrection; go to it and pray in it, for a prayer in it is like a thousand prayers elsewhere. She said: O Messenger of Allah, what if we cannot bear the journey to it? He said: Then let him gift oil to be lit in it; whoever gifts anything to it is like one who prayed therein for Allah. I say: Amr and his shaykh are both very weak, and this chain is mistaken; Ziyad ibn Abi Sawdah only narrated it from his brother Uthman, from Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her) - who is not the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - so Yahya or Amr confused its chain; and it is with Abu Dawud and Ibn Majah in the correct form."
"1615","13 - Book of sales. 1 - Chapter on the merit of leniency in selling and demanding payment. 1335 - Shabbabah ibn Sawwar al-Mada'ini informed us, Hisham - that is Ibn al-Ghaz - narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn, who said: Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) bought a garden from a man, and bargained until the price was settled, then said: Give me your hand - for they would not deem a sale binding except by handshake - and when the seller saw that, he said: No, by Allah, I will not sell it until you add ten thousand. So Uthman turned to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with them both) and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah Almighty admits to Paradise a man who was lenient as a seller, as a buyer, as a payer, and as a claimant. Then he said: Take the ten thousand, so that I earn this word I heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This is a good mursal report, supported by the one after it."
"1616","1336 - Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani informed us, Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Matar al-Warraq, who said: Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) came for Hajj, and when he completed his Hajj he came to a land in Ta'if, and there was a land next to his land which he sought, and there was a difference of ten thousand in the price. When Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) put his foot in the stirrup, he said to a man from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Did you hear the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: May Allah have mercy on a servant lenient in selling, lenient in buying, lenient in paying, lenient in demanding? The man said: Yes. So Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Bring the man back to me; and he gave him the ten thousand and took the land. This is a good mursal report supported by the one before it, each strengthened by the other due to the difference of the two chains."
"1617","1337 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Salim al-Khayyat narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him), that he bargained with a man over a land until the sale became binding or nearly so, and the man said: By Allah, I will not give it to you until you add ten thousand. So Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) turned to a man and said: Do you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on a man lenient in demanding, lenient in claiming? They said: Yes. So he added ten thousand and took the land."
"1618","1338 - Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Harb ibn Surayj narrated to us, a man from Bal'adawiyyah narrated to me, my grandfather narrated to me, who said: I set out to Madinah and alighted at the valley, and there were two men with a single goat between them, and the buyer was saying to the seller: Deal with me fairly; and there was a man of fine stature, so the buyer said: O Messenger of Allah, tell him to deal fairly with me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stretched his hand and said: You own your wealth; I hope to meet Allah Almighty on the Day of Resurrection with no one demanding anything from me for injustice in wealth, blood, or honor, except for its due right. May Allah have mercy on a man easy in selling, easy in buying, easy in taking, easy in giving, easy in demanding."
"1619","2 - Chapter on sale by mutual consent and the permissibility of exchange by giving. 1339/1 - Ishaq and Abd ibn Humayd together; Ishaq said: I was informed, and Abd said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us, al-Mukhtar - that is Ibn Qani' al-Tammar - narrated to us, from Abu Matar, who said: I came out of the mosque and a man called behind me: Lift your lower garment, for it is more God-fearing for your Lord and cleaner for your garment, and take from your head if you are a Muslim. So I walked behind him while he wore a waist-wrap and a cloak and carried a stick, and I said: Who is this? It was said: Ali, the Commander of the Believers - then he mentioned the hadith, and in it: then he came to a cloth-seller's shop and said: O shaykh, sell me well a shirt for three dirhams. When he recognized him he did not buy anything from him. Then he went to another, and when he recognized him he did not buy anything from him. Then he went to a young lad and bought a shirt from him for three dirhams, and wore it from between the wrists to the ankles. Then the owner of the garment came, and it was said: Your son sold the Commander of the Believers a shirt for three dirhams. He said: Why did you not take two dirhams from him? So he took the dirham, then brought it to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) while he sat with the Muslims, and said: Keep this dirham. He said: What is the matter with it? He said: It was a shirt worth two dirhams - meaning your son sold it to you for three dirhams. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: He sold me with his consent, and took with his consent."
"1620","1339/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Ammar narrated to us, al-Mu'afa ibn Imran narrated to us, Mukhtar al-Tammar narrated to us with it. The hadith of Tariq ibn Abdullah al-Muharibi, indicating the validity of exchange by giving, will come, if Allah Almighty wills, shortly in the chapter on measuring upon the one who takes in full and the validity of exchange by giving."
"1621","3 - Chapter on urging vigilance in trade. 1340 - Abu Ya'la reported: Kamil narrated to us, Abu Hisham al-Qannad narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated to us, who said: The cheated one is neither praised nor rewarded."
"1622","4 - Chapter on craftsmen and their earnings. 1341 - Abu Ya'la said: Abdullah ibn Salih ibn Abd al-Samad informed us, Qasim narrated to us, from al-Ala, from a man, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave his maternal aunt a slave boy, and said: Do not make him a butcher, nor a cupper, nor a goldsmith."
"1623","1342 - Jubarah ibn al-Mughallis narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Nahshali narrated to us, al-Haytham narrated to us, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped on the two jugular veins and between the shoulders, and gave the cupper his wage; had it been unlawful he would not have given it to him."
"1624","1343 - Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Adhrami narrated to us, Ali ibn Yazid al-Suda'i narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Nabhan, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade singing women, wailing women, and buying them, selling them, and trading in them. He said: Their earnings are unlawful."
"1625","1344 - Mus'ab narrated to us, Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, from Mus'ab ibn Thabit, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Blessed and Exalted, loves that when one of you does a deed, he perfects it."
"1626","5 - Chapter on warning against unlawful earnings and encouraging lawful earnings. 1345 - Al-Harith reported: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, al-Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbih narrated to us, from Abu A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us - then he mentioned a long hadith, in which he said: Whoever earns unlawful wealth, Allah will not accept charity from him, nor manumission, nor Hajj, nor Umrah; and Allah Almighty records sins against him to that extent, and what remains at his death will be his provision to the Fire. Whoever knowingly buys treachery is like the one who committed it in its shame and sin. And whoever knowingly buys stolen goods is like the one who stole them in their shame and sin - the rest of the hadith. It is fabricated with this chain, though its ending has been narrated with another chain."
"1627","1346/1 - And Muhammad ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us. 1346/2 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Qabisah narrated to us; both said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mus'ab ibn Muhammad - and in the narration of Qabisah ibn Mina from a shaykh of the Ansar, and in the narration of Waki' from a man of the people of Madinah - who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever knowingly buys stolen goods shares in its sin and shame."
"1628","1347/1 - Abu Ya'la reported: Zuhayr narrated to us, Hasan narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Darraj, from Abu al-Haytham, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man who earns wealth from lawful means, then feeds himself or clothes those below him among Allah's creation, it counts as charity for him. 1347/2 - Amr ibn al-Harith corroborated him from Darraj; Ibn Hibban transmitted it through his chain."
"1629","1348 - And al-Tayalisi said: Ja'far narrated to us, from al-Nadr ibn Humayd, from Abu al-Jarud, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let not impress you a man with broad forearms who sheds blood, for Allah Almighty has for him a slayer who never dies; and let not impress you a man who earns wealth from unlawful means, for if he spends it or gives it in charity it will not be accepted from him, if he leaves it it will not be blessed for him, and if anything of it remains it will be his provision to the Fire."
"1630","1349 - And Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, al-Khalil narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Richness is not from abundance of goods, but richness is richness of the soul."
"1631","6 - Chapter on encouraging avoidance of doubtful matters. 1350 - Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, Ubayd ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, al-Ala ibn Tha'labah narrated to us, from Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the mosque of al-Khayf, and his Companions said to me: Step aside, O Wathilah - meaning move away from his face - so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave him, for he only came to ask. So I drew near and said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, O Messenger of Allah, give us a ruling on a matter we may take from you after you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Consult your soul. I said: How can I know that? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave what makes you doubt for what does not make you doubt, even if the muftis give you verdicts. I said: How can I know that? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Put your hand on your heart, for the heart finds rest in the lawful and does not find rest in the unlawful; and the piety of a Muslim is to leave the small lest he fall into the major. I said: Who is the greedy one? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who seeks earnings in unlawful ways. I said: Who is the pious one? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who stops at doubtful matters. I said: Who is the believer? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one whom people trust with their wealth and blood. I said: Who is the Muslim? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe. I said: Which jihad is best? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A word of truth before a tyrannical ruler. Al-Ala ibn Tha'labah is unknown, as Abu Hatim said, but the text has scattered supporting witnesses."
"1632","7 - Chapter on selling until the time of the stipend for soldiers. 1351 - Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir informed us, my father narrated to us, I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrating, saying: Abu Ubaydah ibn Muhammad ibn Ammar ibn Yasir narrated to me, who said: al-Rubayyi' bint Mu'awwidh ibn Afra narrated to me, she said: I entered with some Ansari women upon Asma bint Makhramah, the mother of Abu Jahl - and her son Abdullah ibn Abi Rabi'ah used to send her perfume from Yemen which she would sell until the time of the stipends. She said: So I bought from her and she weighed for me, and I put it in bottles like the others, and she said to me: Write down my due against you; so I said - meaning to the scribe -: Write it against al-Rubayyi' bint Mu'awwidh ibn Afra. She said: You are like the daughter of the killer of his master. I said: By Allah, I am not the daughter of the killer of his master, but the daughter of the killer of his slave. She said: By Allah, I will never sell you anything. I said: And I, by Allah, will never buy anything from you; by Allah, there is neither perfume nor fragrance there. Then she said: By Allah, O my son, I never smelled a perfume more fragrant than it, but when she said what she said she was angry, so I said what I said."
"1633","8 - Chapter on trade in cloth. 1352 - Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn Ar'arah al-Sami narrated to us at Basra, Ibrahim ibn Abdullah al-Razi narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Nuh narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people of Paradise do not trade among themselves; and if they traded, they would trade only in cloth."
"1634","9 - Chapter on blessing in the early morning. 1353 - Abu Ya'la reported: Ammar Abu Yasir narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ziyad narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Yusuf ibn Abdullah ibn Salam, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, bless my nation in its early mornings."
"1635","1354 - And Ammar Abu Yasir narrated to us, Adi ibn al-Fadl and Muhammad ibn Anbasah narrated to us, both said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, bless my nation in its early mornings."
"1636","1355 - And Ahmad ibn Isa narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Usamah, from Ubayd ibn Nistas, freedman of Kathir ibn al-Salt, who narrated to him from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Richness is not from abundance of goods, but richness is richness of the soul; and Allah Almighty gives His servant what is written for him of provision, so be moderate in seeking: take what is lawful and leave what is unlawful."
"1637","1356 - And al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, Wahb ibn Wahb narrated to us, Abbad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty sends down provision according to the need, and sends down patience according to the calamity. Abd al-Aziz corroborated him from Tariq, with al-Bazzar."
"1638","10 - Chapter on sharecropping. 1357 - Ishaq reported: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan informed us, Abu Salamah narrated to us, who said: I asked Abu Ja'far - meaning Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Husayn (may Allah be pleased with them both) - what is mukhabarah? He said: Sharing by division."
"1639","1358 - Jarir informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay', from Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with them both), both said: A man owned a land, and a man said to him: Will you sharecrop with me, so whatever Allah brings forth of anything will be between me and you? He said: Yes, until I ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he went to Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and they said: Ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he asked him and he gave him no answer. He said to them: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me no answer. They said to him: Go on, for had it been unlawful he would have forbidden you. So he sharecropped until the crop stirred or turned green, and it lay on the path of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he passed by it one day and said: Whose land is this? They said: So-and-so's; so-and-so sharecropped it with him. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Call them both. So they both came, and he said to the landowner: Return to this one what he spent on your land, and yours is what your land produced. They did not transmit it with this wording."
"1640","1359 - And Abu Bakr said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, from Abu Ja'far al-Khatmi, who said: My uncle sent me with a lad of his to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, and he said: What do you say about sharecropping? He said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) saw no harm in it, until he narrated a hadith about it from Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him): that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Banu Harithah and saw crops in the land of Zuhayr, and said: How fine is the land of Zuhayr? They said: It does not belong to Zuhayr. He said: Is it not the land of Zuhayr? They said: Yes, but he sharecropped it with so-and-so. So he said: Return his expenses to him, and take your crop. Rafi' (may Allah be pleased with him) said: So we took our crop and returned his expenses to him."
"1641","1360 - And Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay', from Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade renting out crops and hiring, except that a man buys land or it is lent to him. He said: So my father lent a land and he cultivated it and built a house in it; my father rode by one day and saw the building and said: What is this? He said: The one to whom you lent your land built it. He said: As compensation for what I lent him? So he ordered the building demolished. This chain is partly sound mursal and partly stopped."
"1642","1361 - And Abu Ya'la said: Mus'ab ibn Abdullah narrated to us, Hisham ibn Abdullah ibn Ikrimah narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek provision in the hidden parts of the earth. Hisham alone narrated it from Hisham."
"1643","1362 - Musaddad reported: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Muhammad, who said: A man took a land from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), stipulating that he would not put dung in it; he said: Nothing but that sets it right. He said: If nothing but that sets it right, then leave it."
"1644","11 - Chapter on the broker and that a townsman should not sell for a Bedouin. 1363 - Abu Ya'la reported: al-Qawariri narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Salim Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, from a shaykh of Banu Tamim, who said: He sat with me while I was in the mosque of Basra in the time of al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf, with a staff in his hand and a sheet he carried in his hand, then he mentioned a hadith in which: I entered Madinah with my father while a young lad, with camels we had brought to Madinah to sell. He said: Talhah ibn Ubaydullah (may Allah be pleased with him) was a friend of my father, so we alighted with him, and my father said: O Abu Muhammad, go out with us and sell our camels for us, for we have no knowledge of this market. He said: As for selling for you, no; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a townsman to sell for a Bedouin; but I shall go out with you both to the market, and if I approve of a buyer for you I shall order you to sell to him. He said: So he went out with us and sat at the side of the market, and men bargained with us for our camels until a man offered us what satisfied us, so we came to him and consulted him about selling to him. He said: Sell to him, for I am satisfied with his fulfillment and prayer. He said: So we sold to him and took what was ours."
"1645","12 - Chapter on usury. 1364 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, from a man of the people of Basra, from al-Hasan, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a gathering of the Ansar on Thursday night in Ramadan - and he did not fast another Ramadan after it - saying: Barley for barley, a qafiz for a qafiz, hand to hand; and whatever is extra is usury - the hadith. Its basis is in Sahih Muslim without this wording and without this addition."
"1646","1365 - Abu Ya'la reported: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Sukayn - that is Ibn Abd al-Aziz - narrated to us, from Abd al-Mu'min, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gold for gold, silver for silver, wheat for wheat, barley for barley, salt for salt, dates for dates, like for like, measure for measure; whoever adds or seeks addition has committed usury."
"1647","1366 - Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, al-Ash'ath narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, who said: I asked Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) about wheat for dates with excess, hand to hand? He said: In the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we used to buy a sa' of wheat for six sa's of dates, hand to hand; but if it is of one kind, there is no good in it except like for like."
"1648","1367 - Musaddad reported: Hafs - that is Ibn Ghiyath - narrated to us, from Abu al-Awwam al-Basri, from Ata, who said: Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to sell palm trees from his slaves for a year, two years, and three years, and Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) spoke to him about that, and he said: Did you not know there is no usury between a slave and his master?"
"1649","1368 - And Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Abu Ma'bad, who said: Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to sell fruit from his slaves before it was edible, and he saw no usury between him and his slave."
"1650","1369/1 - Ishaq reported: Ya'la ibn Ubayd informed us, from Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib, that he narrated to them, from Salamah ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Rafi', freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: We fell into need, so I took my wife's two anklets in the year Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was made caliph; Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) met me and said: What is this? I said: The clan needed expenses. He said: I have silver with which I want gold. So he called for the scale and put the two anklets in one pan and the silver in the other, and the two anklets outweighed by about a daniq, so he cut it off. I said: O caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it is lawful for you. He said: O Abu Rafi', if you deem it lawful, Allah Almighty does not deem it lawful; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Gold for gold weight for weight, and silver for silver weight for weight; the excess and the one given excess are in the Fire."
"1651","1369/2 - And al-Nadr ibn Muhammad informed us, from Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib, from Ibn al-Sa'ib, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Abu Rafi' (may Allah be pleased with him) with this hadith likewise."
"1652","1369/3 - And Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated to us, and he mentioned it."
"1653","1369/4 - And al-Harith said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib al-Kalbi narrated to us, and he mentioned it."
"1654","1369/5 - And Abu Ya'la said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, al-Kalbi informed us with it."
"1655","1370 - And Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, from Musa ibn Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi, from his father, from Abu Qays, who said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to the commanders of the armies in Syria: You have descended upon the land of usury, so do not buy gold for gold except weight for weight, nor silver for silver except weight for weight, nor food for food except measure for measure. I say: Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib is Ibn al-Kalbi, abandoned outright. Ishaq transmitted his hadith because it has a basis, and he cited as witness the stopped report after it, saying: its chain is unobjectionable; and Abu Qays I think is the freedman of Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him), Ulayy ibn Rabah heard from him, and I do not know whether he heard Abu Bakr or read that in his letter."
"1656","1371 - Ishaq reported: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith informed us, Zayd ibn Murrah Abu al-Mu'alla narrated to us, Abu Sa'id al-Raqashi narrated to us, who said: Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), came to Basra and we sat with him in the congregational mosque, and he said: Will you not forbid this shaykh of yours - meaning al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan - who claims that what people trade hand to hand of silver for silver and gold for gold is unlawful, while I testify that Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) deemed it lawful. Abu Sa'id said: So I said to him: Woe to you! Do you not know that I sat at his head while you were at his feet, then a man came and stood over you, and I said: What do you need? He said: I wanted to ask Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) about gold for gold. I said: Go, for he claims there is no harm in it. Then he uncovered his turban from his face, and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) sat up and said: I seek Allah's forgiveness; by Allah, I used to think that whatever Muslims trade hand to hand was lawful. I heard Abdullah ibn Umar and Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with them both) - they memorized on that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what I did not memorize - and I seek Allah's forgiveness."
"1657","1372 - Jarir informed us, Salim ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) three days before his death saying: I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him from currency exchange."
"1658","1373 - Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us, Isma'il ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Sufayr informed us, Ata narrated to me, who said: A dozen or so of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) and said: We are older than you and more knowledgeable about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than you; what do you think when you deem currency exchange lawful, while we heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding it? Then he mentioned the hadith from Usamah (may Allah be pleased with him). It is in the Sahih, though they did not transmit this wording from this number of Companions (may Allah be pleased with them); and there is criticism of Isma'il."
"1659","1374 - Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani informed us, Ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Qatadah, who said: I asked Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib about a sheep for two sheep until the time of livelihood, and he said: A man asked Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The last thing Allah Almighty revealed was the verse of usury, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away before explaining it, so leave usury and doubt. I say: Ibn Majah narrated it apart from the question."
"1660","1375/1 - And Ishaq also said: Jarir informed us, from Mansur, from Abu Wajzah al-Sa'di, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I had inferior dates, so I bought with them from the market better dates for half their measure. I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him what I did, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go, take your dates and return these. So I did, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dates for dates like for like, wheat for wheat like for like, barley for barley like for like, salt for salt like for like, gold for gold weight for weight, silver for silver weight for weight; whatever is extra is usury. It has a witness in the Sahih from the hadith of Ubadah ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with him); and this chain is fair, except that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib did not hear from Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1661","1375/2 - And Abu Ya'la narrated it: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us with it."
"1662","1376 - Zuhayr narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Masruq, from Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had dates with me, so I found better dates, two sa's for a sa', and I bought them, then brought them to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Where did you get this from, O Bilal? I said: I bought them, a sa' for two sa's. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Return our dates to us."
"1663","1377 - And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Hammad narrated to me, from Abu Salih, from Shurayh, who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: A dirham for a dirham, and whatever is extra between them is usury."
"1664","1378 - And al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbih narrated to us, from Abu A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and mentioned a long hadith - and in it: Whoever consumes usury, Allah will fill his belly with Fire to the extent he consumed; and if he earns wealth from it, Allah will accept none of his deeds, and he remains under the curse of Allah Almighty and His angels so long as a qirat of it remains with him."
"1665","1379 - Yahya ibn Hashim narrated to us, Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, who said: Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) had worm-eaten dates, so he sold two sa's for a sa' - the hadith."
"1666","1380 - And Musaddad said: Mulazim narrated to us, Zufar ibn Yazid narrated to us, from his father Yazid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Suhaymi - who was among the companions of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) - who said: I asked him - meaning Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) - about buying a sheep for two sheep on credit, and he said: No, except hand to hand."
"1667","1381 - Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: If he cannot separate the gold from the silver, there is no harm in selling it for gold or silver."
"1668","1382 - Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Ali and Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them both) forbade currency exchange."
"1669","1383 - Yahya narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) forbade currency exchange."
"1670","1384 - And Yahya narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn Kulthum, from his father, from Mujahid, from Abu Abdullah, who said: I saw thirty of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), all forbidding currency exchange; among them Mu'adh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1671","1385 - He said: And Aws ibn Abdullah narrated to me, Burayd ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: that he met Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) and asked him about currency exchange, and he said: There is no harm in it. Then it reached him that he retracted that view."
"1672","1386 - Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Ash'ath ibn Sulaym narrated to me, from Ubayd ibn Nadlah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about a portion of a slaughtered camel sold for offspring, and he forbade them from it."
"1673","1387 - Yahya narrated to us, from Hisham, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, from one who heard Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) asked about a camel for two camels on credit, and he said: The increase is valid, some of it for some; but as for laid-out meat, there is no harm in it."
"1674","1388 - Yahya narrated to us, from Malik, Salih ibn Kaysan narrated to me, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali, who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) sold a camel for two camels together on credit."
"1675","1389 - Yahya narrated to us, from Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling animals for animals."
"1676","1390 - And al-Tayalisi said: al-Rabi' ibn Subayh narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Silver for silver, gold for gold, dates for dates, wheat for wheat, barley for barley, salt for salt, identical for identical, or weight for weight - the hadith."
"1677","1391 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha, from Ubayd, who said: I slaughtered a camel in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and divided it into portions, and a man said: Give me a portion of the portions for a sheep. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is not valid."
"1678","1392 - And Abu Bakr said: Yahya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Nadrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Dates for dates more deserve to be usury than silver for silver."
"1679","13 - Chapter on measuring upon the one who takes in full and the validity of exchange by giving. 1393/1 - Abu Bakr said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Ziyad ibn Abi al-Ja'd, Abu Sakhrah Jami' ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Tariq ibn Abdullah al-Muharibi (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) twice - then he mentioned the hadith; he said: We came in a company from al-Rabadhah until we alighted near Madinah, with a litter-woman of ours. He said: While we sat, a man wearing two white garments came to us, greeted, and we returned it. He said: From where has the company come? We said: From al-Rabadhah and south of al-Rabadhah. He said: And with us was a red camel. He said: Will you sell me the camel? We said: Yes. He said: For how much? We said: For such-and-such sa's of dates. He did not bargain anything down, and said: I have taken it. Then he took the camel's head until he entered Madinah and disappeared from us, and we blamed one another, saying: You gave your camel to a man you do not know. The litter-woman said: Do not blame yourselves; I saw a face that would never betray you - I never saw a man more like the moon on the night of fullness than his face. In the evening a man came and said: Peace be upon you; I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to you, and he commands you to eat until you are full and to measure until you take in full. So we ate until we were full, and measured until we took in full. Next morning we entered Madinah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood on the pulpit addressing the people."
"1680","1393/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Wasiti narrated to us, Sinan ibn Harun, brother of Sayf, narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Ziyad ibn Abi al-Ja'd, Abu Sakhrah narrated to me, who said: A man of us called Tariq said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) twice. The first time I saw him at the market of Dhu al-Majaz on a mount with bleeding hocks - then he mentioned the hadith. He said: Then after that we came and alighted at Madinah, and a man came out to us and said: From where have you come? We said: From al-Rabadhah or its surroundings. He said: Have you anything to sell? We said: Yes, this camel. He said: For how much? We said: For such-and-such wasqs of dates. So he took its halter pulling it, then entered Madinah with it, and I said: What have we done? We sold our camel to a man we do not know. He said: And with us was a litter-woman at the side of the tent, who said: I guarantee the camel's price; I saw the face of a man like the moon on the night of fullness, who will not cheat you. In the morning a man came with dates and said: I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to you, to eat of these dates until you are full, and to measure until you take in full. He said: So we did."
"1681","14 - Chapter on conditions in sale and assaying dirhams. 1394 - Musaddad reported: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Asim ibn Ubaydullah narrated to me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): about a man who buys a slave-girl on condition that she not be sold nor given away? She said: I disliked that and disliked the condition."
"1682","1395 - And Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Salamah al-Makhzumi, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar, from Zaynab, wife of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) gave me a servant as a gift, and Abdullah said: Will you sell her to me? She said: I said: I would not sell you a servant given to me by the Commander of the Believers. He kept pressing her until he bought her from her, stipulating for her its service until she buys a servant. Then a tale-bearer went and informed Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) of that, so he went to him in the evening or morning, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: I heard you bought Zaynab's slave-girl. He said: Yes. He said: Then do not approach her while anyone has a claim in her. Al-Bayhaqi narrated it through Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn al-Harith ibn Abi al-Dirar, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) gave the wife of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with them both) a slave-girl from the fifth - then he mentioned the like of it."
"1683","1396/1 - And Abu Bakr said: Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abis, from his father, who said: Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) bought a she-camel from two men of al-Nakha', stipulating for them their satisfaction with the coin, then brought them to his house and produced a purse, and they fought over it, then produced another purse and they fought over it. So Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I seek refuge in Allah from you both - then he mentioned the hadith."
"1684","1396/2 - And al-Harith said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to us, and he mentioned it with the wording: Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) bought a she-camel from a man for four hundred dirhams, stipulating for him his satisfaction with the coin. So he brought a man from Isfahan who knew weighing better than him, and Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) produced a purse and he accepted most of it, then produced another purse and he accepted most of it, then produced a third purse and he accepted most of it. Then he said: I seek refuge in Allah from you both - then he mentioned the hadith."
"1685","15 - Chapter on sales that were forbidden. 1397 - Ishaq reported: Isa ibn Yunus informed us, al-Ahwas ibn Hakim narrated to us, from Abu Awn al-A'war, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) sent to Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him) asking him about the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning non-Arab lands, buying them and renting them. So Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling, buying, and renting non-Arab lands. This chain is weak."
"1686","1398 - Abu Ya'la said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Layth, from Ata, from Attab ibn Asid (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him, he forbade him from a loan combined with a sale, from a condition combined with a sale, and from selling what one does not have. This is broken between Ata and Attab, besides the weakness of Layth ibn Abi Sulaym."
"1687","1399 - And al-Tayalisi said: Qays - that is Ibn al-Rabi' - and Sallam - that is Abu al-Ahwas - narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from a man of Banu Tamim, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The price of a dog, the dowry of a prostitute, and the price of wine are unlawful. More on the prohibition of selling wine will come, if Allah Almighty wills, in the drinks section."
"1688","1400 - Musaddad reported: Mulazim ibn Amr narrated to us, Zufar ibn Yazid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Suhaymi narrated to us, from his father - who was among the companions of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): that he asked Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) about buying milk in the udders of sheep, and he said: There is no good in it."
"1689","1401 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling al-majr - meaning buying what is in wombs."
"1690","1402 - And Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, al-Qasim and Makhul narrated to us, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling fruit until its soundness appears."
"1691","1403/1 - And Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us. 1403/2 - And Ahmad ibn Mani': Abu Sa'd al-Saghani narrated to us; both said: Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade buying a debt for a debt, deferred for deferred. And the wording of Waki' - the other said: buying deferred debt for deferred debt, which is debt for debt. Musa is weak."
"1692","1404/1 - And al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys a sheep with withheld milk may keep it three days; if he is satisfied he keeps it, and if he returns it he returns with it a sa' of dates."
"1693","1404/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Humayd ibn Mas'adah narrated to us, Ar'arah ibn al-Birind narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Muslim al-Makki narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not touch-bargain, do not outbid one another deceptively, do not trade in uncertainty, let no townsman sell for a Bedouin; and whoever buys an animal with withheld milk, let him milk it three days - if he returns it, let him return with it a sa' of dates."
"1694","1405 - Abu Ya'la said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Abu Yazid al-Madani, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The runaway is returned - meaning the runaway camel."
"1695","1406 - And Musaddad said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Fruit is not sold until its soundness appears. And Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to say: Until it becomes edible."
"1696","1407 - Khalid narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nu'm (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the miller's measure. This is a good mursal report; al-Daraqutni transmitted it connected with mention of Abu Sa'id through another chain from Abd al-Rahman."
"1697","1408 - And Musaddad said: Sufyan narrated to us, Abu Mu'adh narrated to us, who said: I was a seller of second-hand clothes, and al-Bara ibn Azib (may Allah be pleased with them both) forbade me, saying: This is not lawful."
"1698","16 - Chapter on censure of hoarding. 1409/1 - Ishaq reported: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman informed us, who said: I heard my father say: Abu Nadrah narrated to us, from Abu Sa'id, freedman of Abu Usayd: that Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) used to forbid hoarding. My father said: And they deemed hoarding to be only in food and condiments."
"1699","1409/2 - And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, with the wording: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) used to forbid hoarding, then al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) spoke to him about a freedman of his or a person, so he left him."
"1700","1410 - And Ishaq said: Waki' informed us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to me, from Muslim al-Khayyat, who said: I used to buy dry herbage and date-stones for Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, and he would hoard them."
"1701","1411 - And Abu Bakr and Ibn Abi Umar together said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, al-Qasim narrated to us, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade hoarding food."
"1702","1412 - And al-Harith said: Ubaydullah - that is Ibn Musa - narrated to us, from al-Rabi' ibn Habib, from Nawfal ibn Abd al-Malik, from his father, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade hoarding in the town."
"1703","17 - Chapter on suftajah. 1413 - Ishaq said: Waki' informed us, Abu al-Umays narrated to us, from Ibn Ju'dubah, from Ubayd ibn al-Sabbaq, from Zaynab, wife of Abdullah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her the harvest of forty wasqs of dates and twenty wasqs of barley at Khaybar. Then Asim ibn Adi came to her and said to her: If you wish, I shall pay it here, and take it from you at Khaybar. She said: Until I ask the Commander of the Believers. So she mentioned that to him and he disliked it, saying: What about the guarantee? Waki' said: This is suftajah, and it is disliked in the view of Umar."
"1704","18 - Chapter on advance payment. 1414 - Abu Ya'la reported: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, al-Walid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Hamzah ibn Yusuf ibn Abdullah ibn Salam narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advanced payment to a Jewish man called Yamin for dates to a fixed term. The Jew said: From the dates of the garden of Banu so-and-so? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for from the dates of the garden of Banu so-and-so, no."
"1705","19 - Chapter on land and fruits. 1415 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade muzabanah and muhaqalah - the hadith, with their explanation in it. He said: And I asked Sa'id about renting it for gold and silver, and he said: There is no harm in it."
"1706","1416 - And Sufyan narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, who said: His father disliked selling pasturage in its growing place."
"1707","1417 - Husaym narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan: that he disliked selling fresh dates except cut by cut."
"1708","1418 - Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Burayd ibn Abi Burdah, from Ata: that he was asked about selling fresh dates, and said: One cut, not two cuts."
"1709","1419 - Abu Ya'la reported: Uthman narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sa'igh, from a steward of Sa'd, from Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever withholds surplus water, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, will withhold His bounty from him on the Day of Resurrection."
"1710","20 - Chapter on avoiding doubtful matters. 1420/1 - Ishaq reported: Yahya ibn Wadih al-Ansari informed us, Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Rabadhi narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Ubaydah and others, from Ammar ibn Yasir (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The lawful is clear, the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters; whoever avoids them is more God-fearing in his religion, and whoever falls into them is likely to fall into major sins - like one pasturing beside a sanctuary, likely to enter it; every king has a sanctuary, and the sanctuary of Allah Almighty is His limits. This chain is weak; it has a witness in the Sahih from the hadith of al-Nu'man ibn Bashir (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1711","1420/2 - And Abu Ya'la narrated it: Muhammad ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, Sa'd ibn Ibrahim informed me, from one who informed him, from Ammar ibn Yasir (may Allah be pleased with him) likewise."
"1712","1421 - Musaddad reported: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, al-Hajjaj al-Sawwaf narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, Abu Abd al-Rahman, freedman of Sa'd, narrated to us, who said: I came at night, I and Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him), to a garden of palms, and we sought the garden's owner but did not find him. So Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) said to me: If it pleases you to be truly a Muslim, eat nothing from it. He said: So we remained hungry."
"1713","21 - Chapter on the sale of one in distress. 1422 - Abu Ya'la reported: Rawh ibn Hatim narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Kawthar ibn Hakim, from Makhul, who said: It reached me from Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Verily this time of yours is a biting time, in which the believer clings to what is in his hand for fear of spending. Allah Almighty said: And whatever you spend of anything, He replaces it, and He is the best of providers. He said - and he witnessed the worst of people trading with every distressed person: Verily the sale of the distressed is unlawful; verily the sale of the distressed is unlawful. The Muslim is the brother of the Muslim; he does not wrong him nor forsake him. If you have kindness, give it to your brother; otherwise do not add ruin to his ruin. Al-Kawthar is abandoned, and Makhul from Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) is broken."
"1714","1423 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Ash'ath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam al-Ijli narrated to us, Ubayd ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, al-Ala ibn Tha'labah narrated to us, from Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I showed myself to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the mosque of al-Khayf, and his Companions said to me: Step aside, O Wathilah - that is: move away from the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave him, he only came to ask. He said: So I drew near and said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, O Messenger of Allah, will you give us a ruling on a matter we may take from you after you? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let your soul give you a verdict. He said: I said: How can I know that? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave what makes you doubt for what does not make you doubt, even if the muftis give you verdicts. I said: How can I know that? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Put your hand on your heart, for the heart finds rest in the lawful and does not find rest in the unlawful; and the piety of a Muslim leaves the small lest he fall into the major. I said: May my father and mother be ransom, who is the greedy one? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who seeks earnings from unlawful means. I said: Who is the pious one? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who stops at doubtful matters. I said: Who is the believer? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one whom people trust with their blood. I said: Who is the Muslim? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe. I said: Which jihad is best? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A word of truth before a tyrannical ruler."
"1715","22 - Chapter on censure of cheating. 1424 - Ishaq reported: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani informed us, Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Rabi'ah narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather - then he mentioned the hadith of advance payment, adding: My father said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by rain-wet wheat with dry wheat on top, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What carried you to that? Why did you not leave it so your brothers buy what they recognize?"
"1716","1425 - Al-Harith reported: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbih narrated to us, from Abu A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and mentioned the hadith at length, and in it: Whoever cheats a Muslim in buying or selling is not of us, and will be gathered on the Day of Resurrection with the Jews, for they are the most cheating of people toward Muslims. This is a fabricated hadith."
"1717","1426 - And Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever cheats is not of us. This is mursal, besides the weakness of al-Hajjaj."
"1718","1427 - And Abu Ya'la said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to me, Mu'awiyah ibn Maysarah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, al-Hakam narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Gharazah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man selling food, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O owner of the food, is the bottom of the food like its top? He said: Yes. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever cheats the Muslims is not of them."
"1719","23 - Chapter on the etiquette of selling. 1428/1 - Ishaq reported: Muhammad ibn Ubayd informed us, al-Mukhtar - that is Ibn Qani' al-Tammar - narrated to us, from Abu Matar, who said: I came out of the mosque and a man called behind me - then he mentioned the hadith: and there was Ali (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: So he came to the camel market and said: Sell and do not swear, for oaths sell the goods but erase the blessing. Then he came to a date-seller where a servant-girl wept. He said: What is the matter with you? She said: This one sold me dates for a dirham, but my master refused to accept them. He said: Take them and give her her dirham, for she has no authority. As if he refused, so I said: Do you not know who this is? He said: No. I said: This is Ali, the Commander of the Believers. So he poured out his dates and gave her her dirham. Then he (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by the date-sellers and said: Feed the poor, your earnings will grow."
"1720","1428/2 - And Abd ibn Humayd said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us - then he mentioned the like of it, adding after his saying (and gave her her dirham): For I would love that you be pleased with me, O Commander of the Believers. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: What pleases me about you is that you give them in full. And he added after it his saying: Your earnings will grow. Then he passed by with the Muslims until he came to the fish-sellers, and said: No floating dead fish shall be sold in our market. Then he came to a cloth-seller's house - the market of linen - and came to an elder and said: O shaykh, sell me well a shirt - then he mentioned the hadith, already cited in the chapter on sale by mutual consent."
"1721","1429 - And Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Marwan al-Fazari narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Fadl ibn Mubashshir narrated to us, who said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah does not love the obscene, the foul-mouthed, nor the one who shouts in markets."
"1722","1430 - And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Yazid al-Yashkuri, Abu Hazim narrated to us, who said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) passed by a man selling his ewes, and said: For how much do you sell these sheep of yours? He said: For such-and-such. So Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: I take them for such-and-such. Then he swore not to sell them. So Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) went to relieve himself, then passed by him and he said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, take them for what you offered me. He said: You swore an oath, and I would not help Satan against you by accepting."
"1723","1431 - And Abu Ya'la said: al-Azraq ibn Ali narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, Bishr ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let none of you outbid his brother's sale, nor propose over his brother's proposal."
"1724","1432 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Hisham - that is Ibn Sulayman - narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Muhammad ibn Mihran informed me, that he heard his father say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the Farewell Hajj: O company of merchants, I cast among your shoulders: Do not meet the caravans, and let no townsman sell for a Bedouin."
"1725","1433 - And al-Tayalisi said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from a man of the people of Basra whom the people of Basra call Abu al-Muwarri' and the people of Kufah call Abu Muhammad - from Hudhayl - from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at a funeral and said: Which of you will go to Madinah and leave no idol there but break it, no image but deface it, no grave but level it? So a man of the people stood and said: I will, O Messenger of Allah. The man set out, but as if he feared Madinah and returned. So Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) set out, then returned and said: I did not come to you, O Messenger of Allah, until I left no idol in it but broke it, no grave but leveled it, no image but defaced it. He said: Whoever returns to making any of these - then he spoke sternly about it - and he said to Ali: O Ali, do not be a tempter, nor arrogant, nor a merchant, except a merchant of good; for those are outstripped in deeds. In Sahih Muslim there is a portion on leveling graves and the like."
"1726","1434 - And Musaddad said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Nu'aym ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said: It reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nine-tenths of provision is in trade. And Nu'aym said: The remaining tenth's earning is in grazing livestock - meaning sheep."
"1727","1435 - Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to me, from ... from Abu Sa'id, who said: I heard Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The merchant is wicked, except one who takes the right and gives it."
"1728","24 - Chapter on warning against harsh demanding and delaying payment. 1436 - Al-Harith reported: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbih narrated to us, from Abu A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us - then he mentioned a long hadith, in it: Whoever puts his Muslim brother in hardship, Allah will strip him of his provision, spoil his livelihood, and leave him to himself. Whoever harms a Muslim is not of us, and I am not of him in this world and the Hereafter. Whoever delays his claimant while able to pay bears the sin of a tax-collector. So Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i stood to him and said: What is the sin of a tax-collector? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sin of the tax-collector is that upon him every day is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people; and whomever Allah curses, you will find no helper for him. This is a fabricated hadith."
"1729","1437 - al-Aswad ibn Amir Shadhan narrated to us, Abu Hilal narrated to us, Bishr ibn Numayr narrated to us, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Whoever borrows from people intending, as Allah Almighty knows, to repay it, and his term comes before that, Allah Almighty will satisfy the creditor from his due and pardon him. And whoever borrows from people while Allah Almighty knows he does not intend to repay it, Allah will exact from him, saying: Did you think I would not exact for him from you?"
"1730","1438 - Abu Ya'la reported: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, al-Walid narrated to us, from Abu Abdullah, freedman of Banu Umayyah, from Abu Hazim and Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, I owe the Hajj of Islam, and I owe a debt? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pay your debt."
"1731","25 - Chapter on the merit of lending. 1439 - Abu Ya'la reported: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, Mu'tamir narrated to us, I read to al-Fudayl Abu Mu'adh, from Abu Hariz, that Ibrahim narrated to him: that al-Aswad ibn Yazid used to borrow from a freedman of al-Nakha', a merchant, and when his stipend came out he repaid him; and his stipend came out, and al-Aswad said to him: If you wish, delay for us, for there were dues upon us in this stipend? The merchant said: I shall not do it. So al-Aswad paid him five hundred dirhams in cash, and when the merchant took them, he said to him: Take them. Al-Aswad said: I asked you this and you refused? The merchant said to him: I heard you narrate to us from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Whoever lends twice has the reward of one of them as if he gave it in charity. Ibn Hibban authenticated it, transmitting it from Abu Ya'la with this chain. Ahmad and Ibn Majah transmitted the like of it through Alqamah from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with them both), with a story of Alqamah also, in a different wording, as if they were two separate events."
"1732","26 - Chapter on censure of a loan that brings benefit. 1440 - Al-Harith reported: Hafs ibn Hamzah narrated to us, Sawwar ibn Mus'ab informed us, from Umarah al-Hamdani, who said: I heard Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every loan that brings benefit is usury."
"1733","27 - Chapter on the concession to reduce debt for early payment of deferred debt. 1441 - Ibn Abi Umar reported: Hisham - that is Ibn Sulayman - narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Yazid ibn Rukanah informed me, that Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali informed him: that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the Jews expelled, they said: We are owed debts. He said: Then take and reduce. Ibn Jurayj said: And I was informed likewise from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Abdullah al-Ashhali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1734","28 - Chapter on borrowing, warning against debt, and encouraging patience with the insolvent. The hadith of Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him): Buy upon Allah Almighty, and borrow upon Allah Almighty - will come, if Allah Almighty wills, in the chapter on the merit of jihad. 1442 - And Abu Bakr said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Awf, from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: No debt-burdened person is left in Islam - or he said: debt-relieved. Kathir is weak; the meaning is: no indebted person is left but his debt is paid; it is said: debt burdened him when it weighed him down, and it is also narrated with jim."
"1735","1443 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, al-Ifriqi narrated to us, from Jurayj ibn Sawhi, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Heedlessness is in three things - then he mentioned them. And a man's heedlessness of himself regarding debt until it mounts him."
"1736","1444/1 - al-Hasan ibn Sawwar narrated to us, Layth narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah, from Abu Utbah, that he heard Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) mention that a man died in the time of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving two dinars of debt against him, with no means to pay; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused to pray over him, saying: Pray over your companion. Then Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him) stood to him and said: I shall pay it for him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and prayed over him."
"1737","1444/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Isa narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah, without naming Abu Qatadah."
"1738","1445 - And Abd ibn Humayd said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn al-Walid al-Wassafi narrated to us, Atiyyah narrated to me, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I attended a funeral in which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was present. When it was laid down, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked: Is there a debt against him? They said: Yes. So he turned away from it and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Pray over your companion. When Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) saw him turn away, he said: O Prophet of Allah, he is cleared of his debt; I guarantee what is upon him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came forward and prayed over him, then departed and said: O Ali, may Allah and Islam reward you well; Allah has freed your pledge on the Day of Resurrection, as you freed the pledge of your Muslim brother. No servant pays his brother's debt but Allah frees his pledge on the Day of Resurrection. Then a man of the Ansar stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, is this for Ali alone? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, but for all Muslims."
"1739","1446 - And Abu Bakr said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Sadaqah ibn Isa, who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a man to pray over, and he said: Is there a debt against him? They said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you guarantee his debt, I shall pray over him."
"1740","1447 - And Abu Ya'la said: Sa'id ibn Abi al-Rabi' narrated to us, Isa ibn Sadaqah ibn Abbad al-Yashkuri narrated to us, who said: I entered with my father upon Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), and we said to him: Narrate to us a hadith by which Allah Almighty benefits us. So I heard him say: Whoever of you can die with no debt upon him, let him do so; for I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man's bier was brought, with a debt against him, and he said: I shall not pray over him until you guarantee his debt, for my prayer over him benefits him. They did not guarantee his debt, so he did not pray over him, and said: He is held in pledge in his grave."
"1741","He said: Ammar narrated to us, Yusuf - that is Ibn Atiyyah - narrated to us, Thabit narrated to us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A funeral was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so that he would pray over it. He said: Is there any debt upon him? They said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For Jibril (peace be upon him) forbade me from praying over one who has debt upon him. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The debtor is held hostage in his grave until it is paid off on his behalf."
"1742","Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu Aishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and mentioned a long hadith, and in it: Whoever lends to one in distress and is gracious in seeking repayment, let him begin deeds anew, and he will have with Allah Almighty for every dirham a thousand qintars for him in Paradise. And whoever lends to his Muslim brother will have for every dirham rewards equal in weight to Mount Uhud, Hira, and Mount Sinai. If he is gentle with him in seeking it after it becomes due, charity will be recorded for him for every day, and he will pass over the Sirat like flashing lightning, with no reckoning upon him and no punishment. And whoever his Muslim brother needs him for a loan and he does not lend to him while he has the means, Allah will forbid Paradise for him on the Day when He recompenses the doers of good."
"1743","Abd ibn Humayd said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Kathir, who said: Sad ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) came to collect a debt owed to him by a man, and they said: He has gone out. He said: I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: If a man were killed in the cause of Allah Almighty, then brought back to life, then killed, then brought back to life, then killed, he would not enter Paradise until his debt is paid off."
"1744","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Ubaydah ibn Humayd narrated to us, Abu Maryam narrated to us, from Abu Jafar, from a man of the Ansar - and he was a Badri - who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who would love to be shaded, or that Allah shades him, from the scorching heat of Hell, or from the heat of Hell? The people all said: We do, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship or remits the debt owed by his debtor."
"1745","29 - Chapter: The creditor has the right to speak, and the virtue of one who repays his debt. 1452/1 - Ishaq said: Abdah ibn Sulayman informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought a slaughter-camel or a she-camel from a Bedouin for a wasq of Ajwah dates, and he did not have it with him. The Bedouin came to him demanding payment and he delayed him, so the Bedouin shouted: O treachery, O treachery. The companions of Muhammad restrained him, so he said: Leave him, for the one who has a right has the right to speak. He did that two or three times. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take him to Khawlah bint Hakim - and she was among the first Muhajirat - and borrow from her, for she collects Ajwah dates. So they went to her and she paid him in full. He returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: You have paid in full and paid well. He said: Are not the best of you the best among you with Allah; indeed the best of creation with Allah are those who pay in full and pay well."
"1746","1452/2 - Waki informed us, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) borrowed a camel from a Bedouin, and he mentioned the like of it, and in it he said: The companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rebuked him. And he did not mention: among the Muhajirat."
"1747","Abu Yala said: Abd al-Ghaffar narrated to us, from Ali, from al-Amash, from Abu Dawud, from Buraydah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship will have charity for every day. Then he said after that: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship will have for every day the like of what he granted respite for. Buraydah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is this? He said: That charity is credited for every day before the due date, and charity equal to what he granted respite for is credited for every day after the due date."
"1748","Abd ibn Humayd said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Suhayb narrated to us, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever loves that his supplication be answered, let him relieve one in hardship."
"1749","Al-Tayalisi said: Jafar ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, from al-Qasim, freedman of Yazid, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man was taken to the gate of Paradise, and he raised his head, and behold, written upon the gate of Paradise: Charity is rewarded tenfold, and a single loan eighteenfold; for the borrower does not come to you except while he is in need, whereas charity may fall into the hand of the rich."
"1750","Abu Bakr said: Waki narrated to us, from Zamah, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was pressing a man over two uqiyyas, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured like this, meaning: remit half of it. The hadith. I say: It is in the Sahih without the words: two uqiyyas."
"1751","30 - Chapter on the seller's entitlement to the slave's wealth rather than the buyer. 1457 - Ishaq: Hatim ibn Ismail al-Madani informed us, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Whoever sells a slave who has wealth, his wealth belongs to the seller, unless the buyer stipulates it. And whoever sells date-palms that have been pollinated, their fruit belongs to the seller, unless the buyer stipulates it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged thus."
"1752","31 - Chapter on loans for use. 1458 - Ibn Abi Umar: Hisham narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Abu al-Abbas informed me, from a man of the Ansar, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: A borrowed item must be returned, and a granted milch-animal must be returned."
"1753","32 - Chapter on mudarabah. 1459 - Abu Yala: Abu al-Hakam Misja ibn Musab narrated to us, Yunus ibn Arqam narrated to us, Abu al-Jarud narrated to us, from Habib ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him) when he handed over wealth as mudarabah would stipulate upon his partner that he not travel by land nor by sea, nor camp in a valley with it, nor buy with it a living liver (i.e. livestock), and if he did so he would be liable. He raised his condition to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he approved it."
"1754","33 - Chapter on bankruptcy. 1460 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Hisham narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Abdullah ibn Kathir informed me, from Adiyy ibn Adiyy, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave judgment concerning a person for whom no means of payment was found, while one of his creditors found his loan intact with him, and he judged that he take his goods if he finds them."
"1755","1461/1 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, who said: Muadh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) was a generous young handsome man, among the best youths of his people, and he would retain nothing, and he kept borrowing until all his wealth was locked up in debt. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him to ask his creditors to remit for him, but they refused - and had they remitted for anyone for anyone's sake they would have remitted for Muadh (may Allah be pleased with him) for the sake of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sold all his property for his debt, until Muadh (may Allah be pleased with him) was left with nothing. When it was the year of the conquest of Makkah, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him over a group of the people of Yemen as a governor to compensate him. Muadh (may Allah be pleased with him) remained in Yemen as governor - and he was the first who traded with Allah's wealth - and he remained until he prospered, and until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away. When he came back, Umar said to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both): Send to this man and leave him what sustains him, and take the rest. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him only to compensate him, and I shall take nothing from him unless he gives me. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) went to him, since Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) had not obeyed him, and Umar mentioned that to Muadh (may Allah be pleased with them both). Muadh (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to compensate me, and I shall not do it. Then Muadh met Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and said: I have obeyed you, and I shall do what you commanded me; I saw in a dream that I was in the midst of water and I feared drowning, and you delivered me from it, O Umar. So Muadh came to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and mentioned that to him, and swore that he had concealed nothing from him, until his whip became clear to him. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: By Allah, I shall not take it from you, and I have granted it to you. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: This is when it has become wholesome and lawful. At that, Muadh (may Allah be pleased with him) went out to Sham. The chain of this hadith is sahih, but it is mursal, and they did not include it in their books; rather Abu Dawud included a portion of it in the separate Marasil. Hisham ibn Yusuf differed from Abd al-Razzaq, narrating it from Mamar as connected, saying: from Ibn Kab, from his father; and Ibn al-Mubarak narrated it from Mamar as mursal."
"1756","1461/2 - Al-Harith said: Ishaq ibn Isa ibn al-Tabba narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Mamar informed me, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, who said: Muadh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) was a generous youth, the best of the young men of his people, and he kept on until his wealth was drowned in debt. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke to his creditors, and had anyone been excused for anyone's sake, Muadh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) would have been excused for the sake of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So Muadh (may Allah be pleased with him) remained with no wealth."
"1757","34 - Chapter on reconciliation. 1462 - Abu Bakr said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Ubadah ibn Amr ibn Ubadah ibn Awf, from Abu Ayyub (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Abu Ayyub, shall I not guide you to a charity beloved to Allah and His Messenger? Make peace between people when they hate one another and fall into corruption."
"1758","Al-Harith: Dawud narrated to us, Maysarah narrated to us, from Abu Aishah, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) who both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and mentioned the hadith, and in it: Whoever walks to make peace between two, the angels pray upon him until he returns, and he is given the reward of Laylat al-Qadr. And in it: Whoever defrauds his neighbor of a handspan of land will be collared with it on the Day of Resurrection down to seven earths of fire, until he enters Hell. This hadith is fabricated."
"1759","Musaddad: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah and Ziyad ibn Sad, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The protected area of an old well is fifty cubits, and the protected area of a new well is twenty-five cubits. Said said on his own, not raising it: And the protected area of a cultivated well is three hundred cubits. Abu Dawud narrated it in al-Marasil through al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Umayyah alone."
"1760","35 - Chapter on transfer of debt. 1465 - Al-Harith: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Ismail ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Muslim, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Delay by the rich is injustice, and whoever is referred to a solvent one, let him accept the transfer. Ismail is weak. Al-Bazzar said: He was not followed in it."
"1761","36 - Chapter on the command to return deposits. 1466 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, Sadaqah ibn Yasar narrated to me, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, whoever has a deposit with him, let him return it to the one who entrusted him with it."
"1762","Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu Aishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) who both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and mentioned a long hadith, in it: Whoever betrays his trust in this world and does not return it to its owners dies upon other than the religion of Islam, and meets Allah while He is angry with him, then he is ordered to the Fire, and he falls from its edge for ever and ever. This is fabricated."
"1763","Musaddad: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought a lost deposit, and he did not hold him liable for it."
"1764","Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated to us, from Hilal ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn Ukaym, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) would not hold liable for deposits."
"1765","37 - Chapter on usurpation. 1470 - Abu Bakr said: Waki narrated to us, from Sharik, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Malik al-Ashari (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The greatest misappropriation with Allah Almighty on the Day of Resurrection is a cubit of land stolen by two men and two neighbors, between whom it lies, and one of them steals from his companion, so he is collared with it from seven earths."
"1766","Musaddad: Mutamir narrated to us, from his father, an elder I met in Bahrain narrated to me, from the sermon of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Pilgrimage, that he said: Nothing of a man's wealth is lawful except what he gives willingly."
"1767","Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Kurayb, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cursed is he who diminishes anything of the landmarks of the earth without right."
"1768","Abu Yala: Amr ibn al-Dahhak narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Talib ibn Salm narrated to us, one of my family narrated to me, who said: My grandfather Asim ibn al-Hakam narrated to them: He witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his sermon, and he said: Your wealth and your blood are forbidden to you. The hadith."
"1769","Shabab narrated to us, Awn ibn Kahmas narrated to us, from Atiyyah ibn Sad, from al-Hakam ibn al-Harith al-Sulami, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever takes a handspan from the path of the Muslims will come with it on the Day of Resurrection carrying it from seven earths. He said: And I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in seven campaigns, the last of them Hunayn, and I was marching in the vanguard of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and my mount stopped and would not rise, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while I was beating her, and he said: Gently, and he urged her on, and she stood up."
"1770","Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kurayb narrated to us, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cursed is he who slaughters for other than Allah, and cursed is he whom Allah curses, and cursed is he who diminishes anything of the landmarks of the earth without right."
"1771","38 - Chapter on lost property. 1476 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to me, Abu Farwah narrated to me, Urwah ibn Ruwaym al-Lakhmi narrated to me, from Abu Thalabah al-Khushani, who said: I met him and spoke to him. He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, what about silver found near an inhabited village or a frequented road? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Announce it for a year; if its seeker comes, hand it to him, otherwise keep its container and its tie and its number, then enjoy it. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what about silver found in ancient land? He said: In it and in buried treasure is the fifth. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what about a sheep found in open land? He said: Eat it, for it is for you or for your brother or for the wolf. He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, what about a camel or she-camel found in open land, with its container and water-skin upon it? He said: Leave it; what have you to do with it?"
"1772","Abd ibn Humayd said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, Faid Abu al-Warqa narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa al-Aslami (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I went out and behold the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) sitting, and behold a small boy weeping. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): Take the child to you, for he is lost. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) took him to himself. The hadith. And it will come in full, Allah Almighty willing, in the Book of Etiquette."
"1773","Abu Bakr said: Waki narrated to us, from Sad ibn Aws, from Bilal ibn Yahya al-Absi, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that he picked up a dinar, and cut off two qirats from it, then came to Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her) and said: Make food for us. Then he went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and invited him, so he came with those with him, and he brought them a handful. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw it, he disapproved of it and said: What is this? He informed him, so he said: Pick it up, pick it up; the two qirats are upon me. Put your hands in the name of Allah. This hadith is hasan; Abu Dawud included a brief portion of it."
"1774","1479/1 - Abu Yala said: Sufyan ibn Waki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah ibn Muhammad informed me, who said: Sharik ibn Abi Namir narrated to him, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) brought him a dinar he had found in the market, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Announce it three times. He announced it and found no one who recognized it. He returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, so he said: Eat it, or do with it as you wish. So he bought with it barley for three dirhams, dates for three dirhams, meat for a dirham, and oil for a dirham, and a dirham remained with him - and the exchange was eleven for a dinar - until after that its owner came and recognized it. Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me, and he mentioned that to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If anything comes to us we shall pay it to you. 1479/2 - Yahya ibn Said al-Umawi followed him, from Ibn Jurayj. Al-Bazzar included it and said: This Abu Bakr in my view is Ibn Abi Sabrah, and he is lenient in hadith. I say: Al-Hafiz al-Diya thought he was someone else, so he included this hadith in al-Mukhtarah."
"1775","Abu Yala also said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Umm Abdullah bint Nabil - whose name is Ubaydah - narrated to me, from Aishah bint Sad, from her father Sad (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I was walking with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he found a date-stalk with two dates in it, so he took one date and gave me one date."
"1776","Musaddad: Yahya narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Hamzah al-Araj, a neighbor of his, who said: I heard Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) say concerning lost property: Hand it to the ruler."
"1777","Al-Mutamir narrated to us, who said: I heard Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrating from Ayyub ibn Musa, from his father, or from a man, from his father, that he said to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): I found a dinar and picked it up, until it reached a hundred dinars. He said: Announce it for a year. He announced it for a year, then came to him, and he said: Announce it for a year. Then he came to him in the third time, and he said: Announce it, then do with it as you wish."
"1778","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abbad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Masmul narrated to us, who said: I heard Qasim ibn Mukhawwal al-Bahzi, who said: I heard my father - and he had lived through Jahiliyyah and Islam - say: I said: O Messenger of Allah, we meet camels with milk in them, tied up, while we are in need? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Call the owner of the camels three times; if he comes, well, otherwise untie their hobbles, then drink, then tie up again and leave for the milk its channels. I said: O Messenger of Allah, stray animals come to us; is there reward for us in watering them? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, in every living liver there is reward."
"1779","39 - Chapter on the prohibition of breaking the dinar and dirham. 1484 - Musaddad said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Cutting the dinar and dirham is among corruption on earth."
"1780","40 - Chapter on hiring. 1485 - Abu Yala: Ishaq - that is Ibn Abi Israil - narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give the hireling his wage before his sweat dries."
"1781","41 - Chapter on gifts. 1486 - Abd ibn Humayd said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Mindal narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is given a gift while people are with him, they are partners with him in it. Al-Bukhari mentioned it as a comment."
"1782","Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Said al-Attar al-Himsi informed us, Yahya ibn al-Ala narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, from al-Hasan ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever receives a gift while people are sitting with him, they are partners with him in it."
"1783","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Mubarak, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, Qaylawayh Abu Salih narrated to me, who said: A non-Arab owed me twenty dirhams, and he gave me a gift. I asked Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), and he said: Deduct the price of the gift and take the remainder."
"1784","Abu Yala said: Abu al-Rabi narrated to us, al-Harith narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Rabi, from a man, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Visit one another and exchange gifts, for visiting cultivates love, and gifts remove rancor."
"1785","Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, Hababah bint Ajlan narrated to us, from her mother Umm Hafs, from Safiyyah bint Jarir, from Umm Hakim bint Wada al-Khuzaiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Exchange gifts, for it multiplies love and removes hidden enmities."
"1786","Uqbah narrated to us, Yunus - that is Ibn Bukayr - narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Urwah, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I do not accept a gift from a Bedouin. The hadith."
"1787","Musaddad: Yahya narrated to us, from Malik, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Ghatfan, from Marwan ibn al-Hakam, who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Whoever grants a gift for kinship or for charity, it is permissible; and whoever grants a gift intending reward, he has most right to it if he is not pleased with it."
"1788","Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Mamar narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, concerning a man who gave a young sheep and it gave birth, then wanted to take it back. He said: He takes back its value."
"1789","Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz (may Allah be pleased with him), concerning a man who gave a slave-girl and she grew up, then he wanted to take her back. He said: He takes back the value openly."
"1790","Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from al-Shabi, who said: When a gift is consumed, its owner has no right to return to it."
"1791","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: There was a man nicknamed Himar, and he would gift to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a skin of clarified butter and a skin of honey. When its owner came demanding payment, he would bring him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and say: O Messenger of Allah, give this one the price of his goods. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would do no more than smile and order that he be given."
"1792","42 - Chapter on encouraging equality among children in gifts. 1497 - Al-Harith: Abdullah ibn Awn narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Said ibn Yusuf al-Rahbi, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Treat your children equally in gifts; had I favored anyone I would have favored women."
"1793","43 - Chapter on pledges. 1498/1 - Ishaq: Waki informed us, Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Rabadhi narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Qusayt, from Abu Rafi (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A guest came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent me to a Jew, saying: Tell him: The Messenger of Allah says: Sell to me, or lend to me until Rajab. I came to him and said that to him, and he said to him: By Allah, I shall neither sell to him nor lend to him except against a pledge. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he said: By Allah, had he sold to me or lent to me I would have repaid him. I am trustworthy in heaven, trustworthy on earth. Take my iron coat of mail to him. He said: Then there was revealed consoling him about this world: And do not extend your eyes toward what We have given enjoyment with to spouses among them, the flower of worldly life."
"1794","1498/2 - Abu Bakr said: Waki narrated to us with this. 1498/3 - Ishaq said: Rawh ibn Ubadah informed us - meaning from Musa - with this chain, the like of it."
"1795","1498/4 - Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr narrated to us and mentioned it."
"1796","1498/5 - He said: Al-Hasan ibn Shabib narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, Jafar ibn Ali ibn Abi Rafi narrated to us, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent and borrowed dates from a Jewish man. The Jew said: No, by Allah, except against a pledge. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am trustworthy in heaven and trustworthy on earth."
"1797","Al-Abbas ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Yusuf - that is al-Samti - narrated to us, Jafar ibn Sad narrated to us, from Khubayb ibn Sulayman ibn Samurah, from his father, from his grandfather, from Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever pledges land for a debt upon him, he pays from its fruit what exceeds its expenses, and from that he pays his debt upon him after reckoning what remains for him for his work and expenses justly."
"1798","44 - Chapter on interdiction. 1500 - Al-Harith said: Ismail ibn Abi Ismail narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Haram ibn Uthman, from Abu Atiq, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no orphanhood after puberty."
"1799","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Saminah narrated to us, Mutamir narrated to us, who said: I read to Fudayl, from Abu Hariz, from Abu Ishaq, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: You and your wealth belong to your father. This is a hasan chain."
"1800","45 - Chapter on the sign of puberty by which accountability occurs. 1502 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: I heard a man in the mosque of Kufah say: I was there on the day Sad (may Allah be pleased with him) judged concerning Banu Qurayzah, and I was a boy, and they doubted about me, but they did not find that the razor had passed over me, so I was spared. And there will come, Allah Almighty willing, in the hadd of theft mention of one who considered it by six spans."
